INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3518,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11245734','What the SARS-CoV-2 Pandemic Has Taught Us About Immunosuppression, Vaccinations, and Immune Dysregulation: The Rheumatology Experience.','Jiang Y, Sadun RE.','2024','reshaped revelations dreadful calamity echoing fare foresight c19 c19 c19 gianfrancesco c19 10mg csdmard tsdmards csdmard tsdmard 9mg gianfrancesco antimalarials tnfi belimumab il17i 23i il12 23i tsdmards strangfeld mctd mctd crmo csdmard tsdmard kearsley fleet c19 c19 amassed nimble warp plaguing spondylarthritis furer biontech tnfi jaki furer penultimate alacrity rollout amassed adept hyperinflammatory baricitinib sarilumab postulates overexuberant underdose countless biopanned ccl15 ccl16 c1qb astounding hyperinflammation hyperinflammatory autoinflammation retrospect facto mysterious hyperinflammatory typified sequela hyperinflammatory hyperinflammatory trbv11 vβ21 vβ21 superantigen oas1 oas2 vanishing mysterious disappearing inundated dwindled saied immunopathogenesis dysautonomia coxiella borrelia exertional unrefreshing refocused etiopathogenesis revolve cousin myalgic reap pathophysiologies cautionary gautret publicized refuted politicization infamous gautret retrospect linings debacle correctable misconstrued nichd t32hd094671 gianfrancesco hyrich adely strangfeld gianfrancesco kearsley fleet tovey mikuls mikuls mikuls wahezi wahezi e46 e59 winthrop eby winthrop eby furer eviatar zisman furer eviatar deepak ary lya e068632 wieske kpj steenhuis e338 e350 heshin bekenstein ziv toplak bonelli mrak perkmann haslacher aletaha chakravarty e60 e75 e36 crooks hyperinflammation e594 e602 seino griesel mikolajewska cd014963 marko pawliczak leligdowicz harhay calfee evidra2200118 karakike giamarellos bourboulis casadevall pirofski balevic dandachi hoetelmans rmw bozzette bastard ifns abd4585 bastard gervais voyer ifns sciimmunol abl4340 riphagen theocharis hyperinflammatory verdoni mazza gervasoni martelli ruggeri ciuffreda bonanomi antiga epicentre toubiana poirault corsia m2094 feldstein nejmoa2021680 bamford ganigara galeotti berganza grewal bharath sajjan bayry feldstein tenforde karatzios albanji baghafar alghanmi shaaban alkashlan sende alzahrani filfilan velusamy vivekanandan romli karuppiah yubbu sancho brodin cobat multisystemic moreews gouge khaldi plassart vbeta porritt paschold rivas trbv11 jci146614 porritt rivas paschold willscher arditi bahar superantigenic hyperinflammation amormino tedeschi paldino arcieri fiorillo paiardini tuosto kunkl superantigen yatim oas eabo3627 annapragada e372 e381 koppel yechiam shahar nissan shavit hamad saied griend straalen wulffraat vastert mha mccorkell choutka jansari hornig iwasaki altmann whettlock arachchillage boyton kirchner clauw gautret lagier parola boulware pullen bangdiwala postexposure semler leither mendel bernatsky askanase axfors janiaud pradelle mainbourg provencher massy grenet lega udupa balevic sadun tarrant balevic hornik desmarais costenbader','Abstract Purpose of Review: This review reflects on the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the field of rheumatology, emphasizing resulting insights related to the risks of viral infections in immunosuppressed patients, vaccine immunogenicity in immunocompromised patients, and immune dysregulation in the setting of viral infection. Recent Findings: During the pandemic, global patient registries provided real-time insights into the risk factors associated with severe COVID-19 outcomes in rheumatology patients. Updated evidence-based recommendations from the American College of Rheumatology (ACR) guided rheumatology practice. Studies on COVID-19 vaccines in immunocompromised populations enhanced understanding of specific immunosuppressive therapies on vaccine efficacy. In addition, the immune dysregulation seen in severe COVID-19 underscored a role for immunomodulation in this and other severe infections. Furthermore, novel post-infectious conditions, namely Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome in Children (MIS-C) and Long COVID, reshaped our understanding of post-viral syndromes and revealed novel pathological mechanisms. Summary: Lessons from the COVID-19 pandemic demonstrate the power of collaborative research. The scientific revelations from this dreadful time will, nonetheless, benefit the practice of rheumatology for years to come. Keywords: SARS-CoV-2, Pandemic, Vaccines, Immunosuppression, Immune Dysregulation, Rheumatology Introduction The COVID-19 pandemic was an unprecedented calamity with echoing repercussions. The rheumatology community collectively worried about immunocompromised patients and how they would fare against a virus with a high mortality rate in patients with intact immune systems. Rheumatologists were among the medical and scientific communities that mobilized globally to understand the biology behind COVID-19 and to rapidly develop novel therapies and vaccines. Indeed, the silver lining of this pandemic has been the emerging science that will continue to shape our understanding of human health and disease. This narrative review reflects on some lessons learned from the COVID-19 pandemic, focusing on the insights that continue to inform rheumatology practice. Risk of COVID-19 in Rheumatology Patients At the start of the pandemic, rheumatologists were immediately concerned about our vulnerable patient population. With foresight, the COVID-19 Global Rheumatology Alliance (C19-GRA) was established on March 12, 2020, evolving from a grassroots initiative into a pivotal data collection and collaboration hub for rheumatologists and rheumatology patients. The C19-GRA registry collected provider entries detailing patients with rheumatic conditions and their outcomes related to COVID-19 [ 1 ]. This registry included over 10,000 entries from over 100 countries ( rheum-covid.org ). The C19-GRA registry yielded valuable insights into factors associated with hospitalization and death from COVID-19. In 2020, Gianfrancesco et al analyzed the initial 600 patients enrolled in the registry to identify factors associated with COVID-19 hospitalization ( Table 1 ) [ 2 ]. They found that compared to rheumatoid arthritis, patients with other rheumatic diseases were not at significantly increased risk of hospitalization due to COVID-19. Intriguingly, disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (DMARD) therapy, as a pooled group, did not confer hospitalization risk. In fact, treatment with a biologic or targeted synthetic DMARD monotherapy just before COVID-19 diagnosis was associated with a lower risk of hospitalization. While this study only identified prednisone-equivalent glucocorticoid dosage ≥ 10 mg/day as conferring risk of hospitalization, the study did not include anti-CD20 medications in its analysis [ 2 ]. A subsequent study in 2021, using an expanded C19-GRA cohort of 3,729 patients, reinforced the association between old age and specific non-rheumatic comorbidities with increased risk of death from COVID-19 ( Table 2 ) [ 3 ]. They also found that moderate/high rheumatic disease activity and certain medications, namely rituximab, sulfasalazine, and prednisolone-equivalent dosage >10mg/day, were associated with increased COVID-19 death compared to methotrexate monotherapy. Table 1. Adult cohort: factors associated with hospitalization with COVID-19 in adults with rheumatic diseases. Factor Adjusted OR (95% CIs) Demographics Female (vs male) 0.83 (0.54 to 1.28) Age > 65 years 2.56 (1.62 to 4.04) Ever smoker 1.18 (0.90 to 1.53) Comorbidities Hypertension or cardiovascular disease 1.86 (1.23 to 2.81) Lung disease (COPD, asthma, ILD, other not specified) 2.48 (1.55 to 3.98) Diabetes 2.61 (1.39 to 4.88) Chronic renal insufficiency/end- stage renal disease 3.02 (1.21 to 7.54) Rheumatic Disease Rheumatoid arthritis Ref Systemic Lupus erythematosus 1.8 (0.99 to 3.29) Psoriatic arthritis 0.94 (0.48 to 1.83) Axial spondyloarthritis or other spondyloarthritis 1.11 (0.50 to 2.42) Vasculitis 1.56 (0.66 to 3.68) Other 0.94 (0.55 to 1.62) Medications No DMARD Ref csDMARD only 1.23 (0.70 to 2.17) b/tsDMARDs only 0.46 (0.22 to 0.93) csDMARD+b/tsDMARD combination therapy 0.74 (0.37 to 1.46) NSAIDs 0.64 (0.39 to 1.06) Prednisone-equivalent glucocorticoids None Ref 1–9mg/day 1.03 (0.64 to 1.66) ≥10 mg/day 2.05 (1.06 to 3.96) Open in a new tab Source: Gianfrancesco et al., 2020, n=600. Bold denotes statistical significance. Table 2. Adult cohort: Factors associated with death from COVID-19 in adults with rheumatic diseases. Factor Multivariable logistic regression: OR (95% CIs) Demographics Male (vs female) 1.46 (1.11 to 1.91) Age ≤ 65 Ref 65 < Age ≤ 75 3 (2.13 to 4.22) Age > 75 6.18 (4.47 to 8.53) Ever smoker 1.21 (0.94 to 1.57) Rheumatic Disease Rheumatoid arthritis Ref Systemic Lupus erythematosus 1.2 (0.70 to 2.04) Psoriatic arthritis 0.75 (0.53 to 1.07) Axial spondyloarthritis or other spondyloarthritis 0.72 (0.34 to 1.54) Vasculitis 0.8 (0.60 to 1.08) Other CTD 0.75 (0.58 to 0.97) High/moderate/severe disease activity (DA) vs remission/low DA 1.87 (1.27 to 2.77) Comorbidities Hypertension or cardiovascular disease 1.19 (0.89 to 1.59) Hypertension and cardiovascular disease 1.89 (1.31 to 2.73) Lung disease (COPD, asthma, ILD, other not specified) 1.68 (1.26 to 2.25) Diabetes 1.38 (0.88 to 2.17) Chronic renal insufficiency/end- stage renal disease 1.67 (0.99 to 2.8) Medications Methotrexate Ref No DMARD therapy 2.11 (1.48 to 3.01) Leflunomide 1.56 (0.9 to 2.7) Antimalarials 0.99 (0.66 to 1.48) Sulfasalazine 3.6 (1.66 to 7.78) TNFi 0.85 (0.52 to 1.36) Abatacept 1.20 (0.61 to 2.34) Rituximab 4.04 (2.32 to 7.03) Belimumab 0.71 (0.19 to 2.68) IL-6i 0.83 (0.38 to 1.84) IL17i/IL-23i/IL12+23i 0.25 (0.03 to 2.04) tsDMARDs 1.60 (0.91 to 2.8) Prednisone-equivalent glucocorticoids None Ref 1–10 mg/day 1.43 (0.98 to 2.98) >10 mg/day 1.69 (1.18 to 2.41) Open in a new tab Source: Strangfeld et al., 2021, n=3729. Bold denotes statistical significance. The corresponding pediatric study using EULAR and CARRA registries showed an increased risk of COVID-19 hospitalization with certain rheumatic conditions ( Table 3 ). Compared to JIA, SLE/MCTD, vasculitis (combined as a single group), and autoinflammatory syndromes were associated with COVID-19-related hospitalization [ 4 ]. It is unclear if the greater risk of these particular diseases is attributable to the more aggressive pediatric disease phenotypes or due to more utilization of anti-CD20 therapies in treating pediatric-onset SLE and vasculitis. Table 3. Pediatric cohort: factors associated with hospitalization with COVID-19 in children and young people with rheumatic diseases. Factor Multivariable OR (95% CIs) Female (vs male) 1.1 (0.6 to 2.4) Age 1.0 (0.9 to 1.0) Condition JIA Ref SLE/MCTD, Vasculitis 4.3 (1.7 to 11) Auto- inflammatory syndromes 3.0 (1.1 to 8.6) Other (CRMO, sarcoidosis, ocular, “other”, or was left blank) 1.7 (0.5 to 5.5) Obesity 4.0 (1.3 to 12) Glucocorticoid use 2.1 (0.8 to 5.6) DMARD csDMARD only 0.6 (0.2 to 1.7) b/tsDMARD only 1.5 (0.6 to 3.6) Combination therapy 0.4 (0.1 to 1.5) Open in a new tab Source: Kearsley-Fleet et al., 2022 Table 3 , n=606. Bold denotes statistical significance. However, the main caveat of using specialty-specific registry data is the absence of healthy controls. Instead, the registries use reference rheumatic conditions, specifically rheumatoid arthritis and JIA, as relative comparators. Based on other studies, though, rheumatoid arthritis does not seem to increase the risk of severe COVID-19 over the general population [ 5 ]. The other major caveat to registry data is reporting bias; severe cases may be more likely to be brought to rheumatologists’ attention. Despite these limitations, the C19-GRA studies showcase the collaborative power of registries in collecting real-time data and informing clinical care as the pandemic unfolded. Development of evidence-based guidelines. Throughout this time, there was considerable uncertainty regarding how rheumatologists could best help their patients navigate the pandemic, balancing the risks of immunosuppression with the risks of untreated rheumatologic disease if medications were held. The American College of Rheumatology (ACR) responded swiftly by forming a COVID-19 Clinical Task Force, which first convened on March 26, 2020, just two weeks following the World Health’s Declaration of COVID-19 as a pandemic; the Task Force included ten rheumatologists and four infectious disease experts and focused on guidelines for care of adult rheumatology patients during the pandemic. By April 12, 2020, the Task Force had completed two rounds of modified Delphi method voting, drafted guidance recommendations, received approval from the ACR Board of Directors, and posted their recommendations on the ACR website [ 6 ]. Based on the best available evidence, these recommendations encouraged patients to maintain the lowest dose of glucocorticoids to safely control their disease but not to stop glucocorticoids, DMARDS, or biologic therapies. The recommendations also offered guidance on holding certain immunosuppressant medications for SARS-CoV-2 exposure or COVID-19 infection. As new evidence became available, the Clinical Task Force re-voted, iteratively, on their recommendations. The ACR’s COVID-19 Clinical Task Force generated three updated guidelines for adult rheumatology [ 6 – 8 ]. In parallel, 7 pediatric rheumatologists, 2 pediatric infectious disease physicians, 1 adult rheumatologist, and 1 pediatric nurse practitioner formed an ACR Task Force focused on providing clinical guidance for pediatric rheumatology patients [ 9 , 10 ]. Collectively, these publications provided rheumatologists with a north star during a time of great uncertainty. Lessons Learned. The rheumatology community responded swiftly, both locally and globally, to identify and answer the critical questions facing our field. Through the newly developed C19-GRA, rheumatologists amassed epidemiologic data to help us better understand our patients’ vulnerabilities. Through the ACR Clinical Task Forces, we showed that we could quickly gather, analyze, and synthesize data in real-time, rapidly offering clinical guidance while staying nimble enough in our recommendations that they could evolve with the evidence. Notably, even though recommendations evolved iteratively, they were sufficiently evidence-based to not require eventual reversal of early guidance statements. Vaccine Efficacy in Rheumatology Patients The warp-speed development of the SARS-CoV-2 vaccines was a pivotal turning point in the pandemic. However, the immunogenicity and efficacy of these vaccines in immunocompromised individuals was a question plaguing rheumatologists. Before the pandemic, randomized clinical trials and high-quality clinical evidence on vaccine effectiveness among patients with rheumatic diseases were lacking. Park et al published the only randomized clinical trials related to the impact of holding immune suppression around the time of influenza vaccination; these publications demonstrated that temporarily discontinuing methotrexate for 2 weeks after inactivated influenza vaccination improves immunogenicity in patients with rheumatoid arthritis [ 11 , 12 ]. The effect of other immunosuppressive medications, in other rheumatic conditions, and with other vaccines, remains poorly characterized. With the onset of the pandemic, these unanswered questions were further complicated by the novelty of vaccine platforms developed for SARS-CoV-2, including the mRNA and adenoviral vector vaccines. Furthermore, initial clinical trials of these new vaccines explicitly excluded the participation of immunocompromised individuals, so real-world studies were needed to address this problem. The mRNA SARS-CoV-2 vaccines were the most widely administered and well-studied in the immunocompromised population. Foremost, the vaccine safety profile was overall similar between the controls and patients with rheumatic conditions [ 13 , 14 ]. However, individuals with immune-mediated inflammatory diseases seem to have a lower response to mRNA vaccine than healthy controls ( Table 4 ), although the effect was not specific to any individual disease [ 13 – 16 ]. Table 4. Association of rheumatic diseases and seroconversion following SARS-CoV-2 mRNA vaccination. Diagnosis Seropositive response after 2 nd vaccine dose (Percent) Seropositive response after 3 rd dose (Percent) Controls 122/122 (100) 45/45 (100) AIIRD patients 610/720 (84.72) 153/186 (82.26) Psoriatic arthritis 163/168 (97.02) 38/38 (100) Rheumatoid arthritis 222/274 (81.02) 59/72 (81.94) Axial Spondylarthritis 70/71 (98.59) 13/13 (100) Systemic Lupus Erythematosus 98/108 (90.74) 14/16 (87.5) Connective Tissue Disease 9/24 (37.5) 8/15 (53.33) Large vessel vasculitis 20/21 (95.24) 3/3 (100) ANCA-associated vasculitis 9/29 (31.03) 5/12 (41.67) Other vasculitis 19/25 (76) 1/4 (25) Open in a new tab Source: Furer et al., 2022, n=720, BNT162b2 mRNA vaccine (Pfizer). Bold denotes statistical significance from the control group. In distinction, certain medications had a profound impact on seropositivity. Based on an Israel cohort of 720 individuals with rheumatic diseases who received the Pfizer-BioNTech BNT162b2 SARS-CoV-2 vaccine, the main risk factors for lower seropositivity following the standard two-dose regimen include older age and treatment with glucocorticoids, rituximab, mycophenolate, or abatacept [ 14 ]; seropositivity could be achieved by providing a third dose in the patients on abatacept [ 14 ]. In contrast, only a small percentage of the patients on mycophenolate, rituximab, or glucocorticoids who had not responded to the second dose did respond to the third ( Table 5 ). Similar results were seen in a study of 3,222 individuals in the Netherlands who received both mRNA and adenoviral-based vaccines; they also found that anti-CD20 and mycophenolate mofetil with corticosteroids are associated with a lower relative risk for seroconversion following the standard vaccine regimens [ 17 ]. The third booster vaccine increased seroconversion in individuals treated with mycophenolate mofetil, while seroconversion in individuals with anti-CD20 therapies did not improve. In a pediatric study of 124 adolescents with autoimmune inflammatory rheumatic diseases, 100% achieved seropositivity, with minimal side effects, after the third dose of the mRNA vaccine [ 18 ]. Table 5. Association of immunomodulatory therapies with seroconversion following COVID vaccination. Therapy Seropositive response after 2 nd dose (Percent) Seropositive response after 3 rd dose (Percent) Controls 122/122 (100) 45/45 (100) Any medication 577/686 (84.11) 139/169 (82.25) GC 92/148 (62.16) 23/33 (69.7) MTX 152/182 (83.52) 30/34 (88.24) MMF 19/30 (63.33) 4/6 (66.67) Anti-cytokine biologics 260/266 (97.74) 61/61 (100) TNFi 174/179 (97.21) 22/22 (100) Anti IL-6 39/39 (100) 13/13 (100) Anti IL-17 47/48 (97.92) 26/26 (100) RTX 47/110 (42.73) 19/47 (40.43) ABA 10/16 (62.5) 8/8 (100) JAKi 43/49 (87.76) 14/15 (93.33) Open in a new tab Source: Furer et al., 2022 Table S2, 2–6 weeks after dose. Bold denotes statistical significance from the control group. These studies showed the efficacy of booster dosing to improve serological response to the SARS-CoV-2 vaccines in individuals on immunosuppressive medications. The main exception is anti-CD20 therapy, which significantly – albeit not surprisingly – hinders seroconversion. This negative impact can be mitigated by maximally spacing vaccines from the last dose of the anti-CD20 monoclonal. Fortunately, in multiple studies ranging from a randomized controlled trial [ 19 ] to a large cohort study [ 20 ] of immunocompromised patients receiving a third primary dose of an mRNA vaccine, important endpoints, such as decreased hospitalization and death, were seen following administration of a third dose. Ultimately, these outcomes are more meaningful than anti-spike protein titers, which only accounts for the humoral response and not the T-cell response. The caveats of these SARS-CoV-2 vaccine studies are worth consideration: First, most studies focused on the mRNA vaccine, which is distinct from traditional recombinant protein and polysaccharide-based vaccines. In theory, many results are likely generalizable to other vaccine platforms, but definitive evidence from head-to-head comparisons is lacking. In addition, the majority of COVID-19 vaccine efficacy studies used serology as the outcome. The finding that anti-CD20 therapies mitigate antibody response to vaccines is predictable based on its mechanism of action. Cellular immunity is also vital for pathogen defense but is less often measured as a surrogate for vaccine efficacy. T cell responses to vaccines appear intact with rituximab [ 14 , 21 ], while abatacept diminishes cellular immunity as measured by cytokine production in CD4 + T cells [ 14 ]. That being said, the penultimate measure of vaccine efficacy is the frequency and severity of breakthrough infections, for which seropositivity remains a useful proxy. Development of evidence-based guidelines. The ACR responded with alacrity to the news that two SARS-CoV-2 mRNA vaccines would obtain FDA emergency use authorization (EUA) in the United States. By November 2020, the ACR had created a COVID-19 Vaccine Guidance Task Force consisting of nine rheumatologists, two infectious disease experts, and two public health physicians. This Vaccine Task Force compiled available evidence regarding SARS-CoV-2 mRNA vaccine trial data alongside published data showing the impact of rheumatologic medications on the immunogenicity of a variety of vaccines. Based on this body of evidence, the Vaccine Task Force members voted on 37 initial guidance statements [ 22 ], resulting in recommendations regarding which patients with rheumatic diseases should be eligible for the vaccines (all meeting EUA age restrictions) as well as when and how to manage immune-suppressing medications around the time of vaccine administration. As the vaccine rollout evolved and as additional evidence was amassed regarding immunosuppressants’ impacts on vaccine response, the COVID-10 Vaccine Task Force continued to re-evaluate data and re-vote on guidance statements, offering a total of five manuscripts [ 23 – 27 ] to help rheumatologists advise patients with rheumatic diseases on how to protect themselves from COVID-19 through vaccination, monoclonal antibody treatments, and prophylactic monoclonal antibodies. Lessons Learned. Certain medications have a far more profound impact on vaccine immunogenicity, including high-dose glucocorticoids, anti-CD20 therapies, mycophenolate, and abatacept. While antibody titers in these patients may never reach the levels of healthy controls, patients on these medications do, for the most part, derive benefit from additional SARS-CoV-2 mRNA vaccine doses, even while they continue on their immunosuppressant medications. This lesson should, and hopefully will, translate to vaccination strategies for other infections, from seasonal vaccines like influenza to routine childhood vaccines for pediatric rheumatology patients on immunomodulatory therapy. COVID-19 and Immune Dysregulation Even as we became more adept at caring for established rheumatology patients during the COVID-19 pandemic, new conditions that called for rheumatologists’ expertise emerged. Immunomodulation for severe COVID-19. Early in the pandemic, it became clear that a subset of patients with severe COVID-19 developed a hyperinflammatory phenotype similar to macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) [ 28 , 29 ]. A randomized clinical trial showed that dexamethasone treatment among hospitalized patients with COVID-19 on respiratory support resulted in slightly lower mortality [ 30 , 31 ]. In addition, a meta-analysis of treatment approaches for severe COVID-19 showed decreased mortality and less need for mechanical ventilation for groups treated with tocilizumab and baricitinib, even while anakinra and sarilumab did not show benefit [ 32 ]. Immunomodulation therapy has since become the standard of care for the treatment of severe COVID-19, which represents a paradigm shift in the clinical treatment of certain infectious diseases, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and sepsis [ 33 , 34 ]. The basic science model known as the damage-response theory of microbial pathogenesis supports this paradigm [ 35 ] as it postulates that either too little or too much immune response leads to host damage. Based on this theory, immune-suppressing therapies may benefit a subset of patients with overexuberant host immunity, where tissue damage predominantly stems from the immune response rather than the infection. It is important to note that patients experiencing cytokine storm may need substantially higher doses of cytokine-inhibiting biologics than are required to control inflammatory arthritis; hence, extrapolating doses from canonical rheumatic diseases could underdose severe COVID-19 patients [ 36 ], or patients with MAS driven by other infections. Notably, the COVID-19 pandemic increased clinician’s awareness of MAS, and providers are now more likely to identify MAS pathophysiology in patients whose cytokine storm is secondary to triggers beyond just COVID-19; it is likely that earlier recognition and treatment has already saved countless lives, and hopefully will continue to do so over the years to come. Autoantibodies against immune proteins in severe COVID-19. Early in the pandemic, a notable discovery was the association between anti-interferon antibodies and severe COVID-19. Autoantibodies neutralizing high concentrations of IFN-α and IFN-ω were found in 101/987 (10%) of patients with life-threatening COVID-19 compared to 0/663 (0%) of patients with asymptomatic/mild infection [ 37 ]. The follow-up study showed that highly neutralizing anti-interferon autoantibodies could be found in 18% of patients who died of COVID-19 [ 38 ]. Moreover, this study reported that anti-interferon antibodies exist with low frequency in the general population but sharply increase with age, possibly explaining age as a significant risk factor for COVID-19 mortality. Another study broadly biopanned for autoantibodies against extracellular and secreted proteins. The study found increased autoantibodies against a broad array of immune proteins after SARS-CoV-2 infection. They reported finding neutralizing antibodies against many cytokines and chemokines, including IL-1α, CCL15, CCL16, C1qB, and IL-13, to list a few [ 39 ]. When tested in a mouse model of COVID-19, some of these autoantibodies increased mortality [ 39 ]. The astounding array of autoantibodies against immune-related antigens impacts our understanding of host-pathogen interactions. Sometimes considered immune “errors,” antibodies directed at elements of the immune response itself appear to be detrimental to the host. Whether these antibodies are indeed “errors” or whether they serve an evolutionary purpose in different contexts remains unknown. Given possible generalizability to other infections, exploring how these anti-immune (not merely autoimmune) antibodies develop and their role in infection severity will be important. Lessons Learned. As the scientific community’s attention turned to understanding why certain patients experience severe COVID-19 syndrome, we have learned much about hyperinflammation and autoimmunity co-occurring with infections. Because of rheumatologists’ experience with managing MAS and immunomodulatory therapies, they have played a significant role in managing these patients, even if they are not known to have underlying rheumatic diseases. Heightened awareness and early recognition of hyperinflammatory states and MAS-like conditions are needed to prevent mortality. Novel Immune-Mediated Diseases Following COVID-19 Historically, infection-triggered autoinflammation and autoimmunity have been challenging to prove. Infections are common and sporadic, may be temporally delayed from the immune-mediated phenomenon, and are difficult to detect in retrospect. During the COVID-19 pandemic, however, a novel infectious agent became the de facto independent variable, which allowed for the observation of post-infectious complications as the dependent variable. The discoveries of MIS-C and Long COVID, both triggered by SARS-CoV-2 infection, constitute paradigm-shifting developments that enhance our scientific comprehension of similar pathologies. Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome in Children (MIS-C). In the early days of the pandemic, a mysterious Kawasaki Disease (KD)-like illness in children was independently reported from multiple continents following local SARS-CoV-2 outbreaks [ 40 – 42 ]. Although acute COVID-19 infection in children is usually mild, the infection can trigger a delayed inflammatory illness in rare cases, progressing in some cases to shock and even death. The temporal association between local COVID-19 infections and the hyperinflammatory syndrome seen in children helped identify MIS-C (known in Europe as the Pediatric Inflammatory Multisystem Syndrome Temporally associated with SARS-CoV-2) [ 43 , 44 ]. In contrast to KD, which is most common in toddlers and school-aged children and more prevalent in children of Asian ancestry, MIS-C is more prevalent among older children and adolescents, disproportionately affecting children of African or Hispanic ancestry. While both conditions are typified by fever, rash, and coronary changes, MIS-C differs from KD in the higher rates of gastrointestinal symptoms and cardiogenic shock [ 45 ]. Fortunately, cardiovascular manifestations in MIS-C appear largely transient, with 91% of left ventricular dysfunction and 79% of coronary artery aneurysms normalizing within 30 days [ 46 ], whereas aneurysms in KD often form later and are a chronic sequela. This hyperinflammatory syndrome that occurs weeks after COVID-19 infection can also occur in adults, albeit less frequently, and is termed Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome in Adults (MIS-A) [ 47 ]. MIS-C and MIS-A can both be successfully treated with a combination of steroids and IVIg, often with escalation to anakinra, infliximab, or tocilizumab [ 48 , 49 ]. The discovery of MIS-C has led to renewed interest in mechanisms driving post-infectious inflammation. Compared to other hyperinflammatory conditions like KD and toxic shock syndrome, MIS-C revealed unique immunological mechanisms implicated in pathogenesis [ 50 , 51 ]. Two research groups observed polyclonal expansion of activated T cells expressing the TRBV11–2 gene , encoding the Vβ21.3 T cell receptor beta chain, in the MIS-C cohort [ 52 , 53 ]. Interestingly, this polyclonal Vβ21.3 T cell expansion does not appear to be directed against SARS-CoV-2 antigenic peptides. Instead, one group has proposed that the viral spike protein may act as a superantigen with this subset of T cells based on in silico predictions [ 54 ], but experimental data to this effect are lacking [ 52 , 55 ]. In addition, sequencing studies have found autosomal recessive deficiencies in interferon pathway genes ( OAS1, OAS2 , and RNAse L ) in ~1% of children with MIS-C [ 56 ]. Myeloid cell lines containing these mutations produce excessive amounts of inflammatory cytokines upon SARS-CoV-2 stimulation [ 56 ]. Though far from a unifying theory, these studies suggest that different genetic and immune risk factors may lead to a convergent disease phenotype. Unfortunately, subsequent studies will have to contend with the vanishing epidemiology of MIS-C. Indeed, as mysterious as its sudden onset, MIS-C has been disappearing ( Figure 1 ). Early in the pandemic, from the summer of 2020 through late 2021, cases of MIS-C inundated pediatric hospitals. With the sharp rise in the delta variant, post-surge MIS-C incidence and severity were surprisingly diminished [ 57 ]. Since the omicron variant became predominant in early 2022, the incidence of MIS-C has dwindled [ 58 ]. Possible explanations for this stark decrease include the rise of protective immunity from prior infection and vaccination, as well as viral mutations leading to reduced pathogenesis. Importantly, in a meta-analysis, Saied et al found that the pooled odds ratio for MIS-C in vaccinated compared to unvaccinated children was 0.04 (95% CI: 0.03–0.06) [ 59 ], meaning the risk of MIS-C is 24 times higher in unvaccinated children. This likely illustrates the importance of first-time infection in the immunopathogenesis of MIS-C, and perhaps KD, as well as the protective nature of immune memory. Figure 1. Open in a new tab The disappearance of MIS-C. Source: modified from the CDC, https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#mis-national-surveillance , accessed on January 10, 2024. The orange line represents weekly COVID-19 positivity, whereas the blue bars represent the weekly number of MIS-C cases, which dropped off considerably by April of 2022. Long COVID. Patient-termed “Long COVID” is characterized by persistence of symptoms following acute COVID-19. Long COVID is estimated to affect 10% of infected individuals, representing millions of people worldwide. It is more commonly reported in adults but can be seen in children. The most common symptoms include fatigue, dyspnea, chest pain, dysautonomia, as well as gastrointestinal and neurocognitive symptoms [ 60 ]. It turns out that the phenomenon of chronic sequelae following infections has long been known but was often medically dismissed due to the nonspecific nature of complaints and the lack of objective findings. These unexplained post-acute infection syndromes (PAIS) have been documented after infections with viruses (Dengue, Chikungunya, enterovirus), bacteria ( Coxiella, Borrelia ), and parasites (Giardia). They share core symptoms like exertional intolerance, fatigue, neurocognitive impairment, unrefreshing sleep, and musculoskeletal complaints [ 61 ]. Long COVID lends credibility to these syndromes and has refocused attention on etiopathogenesis. In fact, a massive mobilization of modern science is underway to better understand Long COVID. The proposed mechanisms are multifaceted but thematically revolve around perturbations of immunity [ 62 ]. The role of rheumatology in Long COVID is still evolving, but at the minimum, it will be to care for patients with rheumatic disease who develop Long COVID [ 63 ]. As basic science clarifies immune dysregulation associated with Long COVID, rheumatology’s expertise in treating immune-mediated diseases makes it well-suited for involvement in clinical guideline development [ 64 ]. Ongoing clinical trials will hopefully inform patient care in this specific disease and, more broadly, similar post-infectious conditions. Lessons Learned. MIS-C and Long COVID have attracted considerable attention from the clinical and scientific communities. Their respective “cousin conditions,” KD and myalgic encephalomyelitis, will likely reap the benefits of the investments we are now making to better understand the pathophysiologies, which will, in turn, inform therapeutic care. Cautionary Tale: Hydroxychloroquine On March 16, 2020, Gautret et al publicized their open-label, non-randomized study of 36 hospitalized patients with SARS-CoV-2 infection treated with or without hydroxychloroquine (HCQ) [ 65 ]. This small study, whose findings would be refuted later by more rigorous studies, would fuel the politicization of COVID-19 therapy for months to come [ 66 , 67 ]. The ineffective use of HCQ for COVID-19 had real-world impacts, including many rheumatology patients facing drug shortages [ 68 ], which led many states to restrict HCQ prescriptions [ 69 ]. Moreover, the use of HCQ to treat COVID-19 increased mortality. A meta-analysis of COVID-19 therapies showed that the odds ratio for all-cause mortality for HCQ was ~1.1 [ 70 , 71 ], often due to arrhythmia, in the setting of doses higher than used in rheumatologic conditions and in combination with other QT-prolonging medications (e.g., azithromycin), such as in the now-infamous Gautret et al paper [ 65 ]. With this statistic, one study estimated worldwide in-hospital deaths related to HCQ use in COVID-19 to be greater than 16,000 [ 72 ]. In retrospect, PK/PD models for HCQ reveal that short-term use of HCQ that starts following an infection would not achieve sufficient tissue concentrations as needed to improve COVID-19 infection outcomes [ 73 , 74 ]. A broader understanding of these principles, early on, might have prevented the inappropriate use of this medication. Lessons Learned. Two silver linings emerged from the HCQ COVID-19 debacle. First, we were reminded that preliminary studies can be fraught with bias that is often correctable with rigor and repetition. Thus, we must not let early results be amplified without scrutiny, because misinformation misconstrued as evidence can have dire repercussions. Moreover, the time it takes to correct the public’s understanding of misinformation far exceeds the time it takes to arrive at scientific certainty. Second, the unfortunate use of HCQ in critically ill populations highlighted the QT-prolonging effects of HCQ. While EKG monitoring was not part of the standard pre-pandemic practice when prescribing HCQ, the clinical wisdom now is to consider EKG monitoring, especially in patients with additional risks for cardiac arrhythmias [ 75 ]. Conclusions The COVID-19 pandemic was an unprecedented global challenge. The rheumatology community, faced with unique concerns for immunocompromised patients, actively engaged in research and collaboration. Lessons learned included the increased risk of infection associated with poorly controlled rheumatic disease and specific medications like rituximab and high-dose steroids. Evaluating vaccine efficacy in rheumatology patients revealed challenges in achieving seroconversion in patients taking high-dose steroids, mycophenolate, and anti-CD20 therapies. In addition, the emergence of immune dysregulation triggered by COVID-19 highlighted the importance of immunomodulation in severe cases and uncovered autoantibody generation triggered by infection. Finally, the novel diseases MIS-C and Long COVID, triggered by SARS-CoV-2, offer distinct opportunities to study post-infectious pathology and inform clinical care for similar diseases. As we move beyond the pandemic, the lessons learned will undoubtedly continue to shape rheumatology practice for years. Declarations. YJ is supported by NICHD of the National Institutes of Health under award number T32HD094671. RS is supported by grants from the Arthritis Foundation, the Lupus Research Alliance, and the Rheumatology Research Foundation. Footnotes Conflict of interest. RS served on the ACR COVID-19 Vaccine Guidelines Task Force. Human and Animal Rights and Informed Consent. This article does not contain any studies with human or animal subjects performed by the authors. References 1. Robinson PC, Yazdany J (2020) The COVID-19 Global Rheumatology Alliance: collecting data in a pandemic. Nat Rev Rheumatol 2020 166 16:293–294 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Gianfrancesco M, Hyrich KL, Al-Adely S, et al. (2020) Characteristics associated with hospitalisation for COVID-19 in people with rheumatic disease: Data from the COVID-19 Global Rheumatology Alliance physician-reported registry. Ann Rheum Dis 79:859–866 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Strangfeld A, Schäfer M, Gianfrancesco MA, et al. (2021) Factors associated with COVID-19-related death in people with rheumatic diseases: Results from the COVID-19 Global Rheumatology Alliance physician-reported registry. Ann Rheum Dis 80:930–942 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Kearsley-Fleet L, Chang ML, Lawson-Tovey S, et al. (2022) Outcomes of SARS-CoV-2 infection among children and young people with pre-existing rheumatic and musculoskeletal 