m (ages 12–21 years): 189 items. Standardized T‐scores are generated via computerized software, with T > 70 indicating clinically significant symptoms 8–21 30 Yes No English, Spanish, and French Open in a new tab * This table has been adapted from Treemarcki et al. 4 Many considerations (eg, age of patients or prior screening practices in a given setting) may drive selection of specific tools, but one example we suggest is the use of the Patient Health Questionnaire‐9 and the Generalized Anxiety Disorder‐7 for screening for depression and anxiety, respectively. Collaborative management statements Delphi panel members reached consensus after two rounds of voting in all but one of the collaborative management guidance statements, and two of these comprised the 11 final guidance statements (Table 1 ). These two recommendations were that patients with moderate or severe symptoms need urgent assessment by a mental health specialist and patients with mild symptoms should follow up with their primary physician for monitoring. Eight additional statements reaching consensus, but with less empirical support, are detailed in Supplementary Table 3 . Clinic environment and education statements Delphi panel members reached consensus after two rounds of voting in all but two of the clinic environment/education statements, and two of these comprised the 11 guidance statements. These two recommendations were that patients with a new PRD diagnosis should be offered resources (eg, websites or peer support) that address common mental health concerns for youth with PRDs and that ongoing discussions and education about mental health should occur in a manner consistent with the patient''s developmental stage and sociocultural background. A summary of resources providers may wish to consider referencing are provided in Table 3 . Table 3. Resources for addressing mental health for youth with PRDs * Name and website For patients and families: educational resources from patient organizations 