 concerns for patients with PRDs through comments submitted by the pediatric rheumatology community during the open comment period and from participants in the Delphi panel. Most comments came from pediatric rheumatologists and overall supported the importance of addressing mental health for pediatric rheumatology patients and the usefulness of providing clinicians with consensus‐supported guidance statements. Additionally, however, some voiced concern about the lack of existing mental health support and resources for pediatric rheumatologists to effectively address mental health in practice. It was noted that mental health education for pediatric rheumatologists would be helpful to discuss mental health concerns and potential interventions. Several comments emphasized the potential for optimizing engagement of primary care physicians in mental health care as an approach for mitigating the lack of access to other mental health specialists, such as social workers, psychologists, and psychiatrists. Other comments highlighted the opportunity for pediatric rheumatology practices to use guidance statements to advocate for integration of these much‐needed specialists into the medical team. DISCUSSION We present the results of a project to develop consensus‐based guidance to empower pediatric rheumatologists to address a current gap in clinical care regarding the mental health of youth with PRDs. The 11 mental health guidance statements endorsed by the ACR are supported by substantial evidence and supported by the North American pediatric rheumatology community. Drawing on the growing literature on mental health in pediatric rheumatology, along with evidence from pediatric chronic disease and general pediatrics, this guidance is anticipated to advance and align increasing efforts to address mental health concerns for youth with PRDs in the pediatric rheumatology setting. All the mental health screening statements, focused on depression, anxiety, and SI in youth aged 12 years and older, achieved consensus in the first voting round. Seven of these statements were also endorsed by the ACR. Given the large evidence base for these recommendations, there is likely more comfort with screening recommendations. Furthermore, mental health screening emerged as a high‐priority item in the CARRA Mental Health Workgroup Prioritized Research Agenda, 16 and there was high support for these practices among the respondents representing the pediatric rheumatology community. It is notable that several screening statements achieving high levels of consensus, but with less empirical support (and therefore not endorsed by the ACR), pertained to an important role of pediatric rheumatologists in interpreting mental health concerns in the context of disease (eg, neuropsychiatric manifestations, medication adherence, and side effects). This highlights a need for further research on the relationship between mental health disorders and disease factors/outcomes, which was identified as one of the highest‐priority topics in the research agenda developed by the CARRA Mental Health Workgroup. 16 All adolescents and young adults receiving ongoing care from a pediatric rheumatology team would likely benefit from universal mental health screening and assessment of contributing socioenvironmental factors. This is important to promote equity in access to care, 38 which is reflected in the endorsed statements. Because mental health screening is recommended to be completed universally in pediatric primary care and other specialty clinic settings, we believe that adoption of this approach in the pediatric rheumatology setting is important and reasonable. However, when resources are limited, pediatric rheumatology clinics may choose to start with particular patient populations (eg, adolescent patients with lupus). Several sites within the pediatric rheumatology community have had success using the Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7‐item scale and the Patient Health Questionnaire 9‐item scale as short self‐report tools for routine assessment of anxiety and depression, although the specific screening tools used may vary based on existing processes underway in pediatric rheumatology practices. Brief clinically validated tools available for use in pediatric rheumatology settings are listed in Table 2 . 4 , 39 , 40 Of note, although our current recommendations are focused on screening youth aged 12 years and older, some validated measures are normed for use with younger children. Encouragingly, mental health screening has gained momentum in pediatric rheumatology, as recent multicenter studies have shown feasibility and acceptability of mental health screening in rheumatology clinics, 17 , 18 and several efforts are focused on using quality improvement and implementation science to implement screening within pediatric rheumatology clinics. 20 , 21 , 23 For pediatric rheumatology clinicians wishing to implement mental health screening, the American Board of Pediatrics Mental Health Roadmap Project has developed educational resources and toolkits for integrating mental health care into pediatric subspecialty practices (Table 3 ; https://www.roadmapforemotionalhealth.org ). Additionally, integration and automation of screening processes within electronic health records may also allow for reliable and minimally burdensome execution, as recently demonstrated in a pediatric rheumatology setting. 41 Most of the collaborative management guidance statements achieved consensus. Two of these ACR‐endorsed statements address interdisciplinary management of mental health symptoms in youth with PRDs through the involvement of mental health specialists. The other management statements reaching consensus reflected additional principles of shared care (patient‐provider) and interdisciplinary care (eg, team communication), handling of SI, and taking a proactive approach to mental health. We recognize that the feasibility of implementing these guidance statements may be limited at specific institutions/sites depending on the level of resources/support for mental health care and from primary care providers. However, it is important to recognize that these management recommendations allow for tailored levels of care based on mental health symptomology as illustrated by the psychosocial preventive health model, 42 which has been adapted for pediatric rheumatology. 4 In this model, tiered support is offered based on levels of need. Because most patients will have mild or moderate symptoms, they may benefit from low‐intensity proactive support with psychoeducation and, if needed, brief counseling or psychotherapy. These supports, particularly psychoeducation and brief counseling, can be provided by a variety of providers, including social workers, 20 , 22 child life specialists, and community/school counselors or psychologists. Additionally, a recent study showed the value of partnering with adolescent medicine specialists to evaluate and manage mental health problems in adolescents with lupus. 43 Because only a small percentage of patients may need urgent and/or intensive intervention, a stratified approach to care can maximize the use of limited resources by reserving higher levels of care (eg, with a psychologist or psychiatrist) for children with more severe mental health problems. 44 Although statements related to the management of those with mild symptoms did not achieve consensus during the first voting round, there may be a role for using additional supports, including a multidisciplinary team in pediatric rheumatology‐based interventions to address mild symptoms and potentially prevent worsening via a proactive approach. This was indeed identified as one of the highest‐priority topics in the CARRA mental health research agenda 16 and was identified by the international task force for development of treat‐to‐target guidelines for childhood‐onset lupus. 45 Importantly, there is an ongoing study of promising approaches with interventions such as cognitive behavioral therapy tailored to youth with lupus and milder mental health symptoms, 46 , 47 and there has been success of similar collaborative efforts to enhance mental health supports in other subspecialty settings. 48 , 49 Additionally, implementation science methods 50 may also allow for training other providers within the rheumatology team (eg, social workers, nurses, or medical assistants) to address mild or moderate mental health symptoms. As research on mental health management in the context of pediatric rheumatology settings continues to emerge, the statements pertaining to mild symptoms will warrant revisiting and future consideration. Among the final selected recommendations, the two pertaining to the pediatric rheumatology clinic environment and education address discussion about mental health with the patient, both soon after PRD diagnosis and in an ongoing manner, to promote awareness and education about common mental health challenges for youth with PRDs. Of course, particularly in the case of minors, parent engagement and support are critical. Mental health education of youth with PRDs and their families can be facilitated by leveraging online resources, including resources that targeted to newly diagnosed youth (Table 3 ). Recent efforts in pediatric rheumatology are also aiming to increase mental health literacy and skills for clinicians supporting care of patients with PRDs through rheumatology‐specific workshops. 51 , 52 Additionally, resources for clinicians available from the American Board of Pediatrics Mental Health Roadmap Project (Table 3 ) include tools to support mental health assessment and management. There are limitations to the application of these guidance statements. First, the screening recommendations do not address concerns for patients younger than age 12 years. Second, our work focused on the opinions of CARRA members, the majority of whom are pediatric rheumatologists. Furthermore, our respondents were largely from North America, and findings may not be applicable to other geographic regions. However, these guidance statements are aligned with ongoing international efforts striving to address mental health for youth with PRDs. These include the international task force for the development of treat‐to‐target guidelines for childhood‐onset lupus 45 and the Psychological Impact Scientific Working Group for the Myositis International Health & Research Collaborative Alliance. 53 Additionally, the 2010 British Society of Paediatric and Adolescent Rheumatology Standards of Care for youth with juvenile idiopathic arthritis specify that every patient should have access to a pediatric clinical psychologist as part of the core multidisciplinary team. 54 There may also be special considerations for addressing mental health care during (often shortened) rheumatology telemedicine visits whereby the patient may not have adequate privacy. However, strategies to adapt mental health practices for telemedicine have been provided by other subspecialties 55 , 56 , 57 , 58 such as psychiatry. For example, mental health screeners may be completed using secure patient portals. Patients may also be informed in advance that a private space is needed for their appointment. Finally, it must be recognized that there are widely varying levels of resources available across pediatric rheumatology practices to address mental health concerns. We recognize that these resource differences cannot be solved by creating guidance alone, and it is crucial for the pediatric rheumatology community to advocate for access to mental health services for patients. 35 In conclusion, the finalized mental health guidance statements are supported by substantial evidence and supported by the North American pediatric rheumatology community. This guidance provides a standardized, evidence‐based approach for addressing mental health concerns for youth with PRDs receiving care in pediatric rheumatology practices. These guidance statements provide a framework to consider local steps needed for improving mental health intervention for our patients and can be used to advocate for resources to enable collaborative interdisciplinary care of youth with PRDs. Although this guidance is intended to evolve as new evidence becomes available, it may be used now to help improve both mental health and health‐related outcomes for youth impacted by rheumatologic conditions. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS All authors contributed to at least one of the following manuscript preparation roles: conceptualization AND/OR methodology, software, investigation, formal analysis, data curation, visualization, and validation AND drafting or reviewing/editing the final draft. As corresponding author, Dr Cunningham confirms that all authors have provided the final approval of the version to be published and takes responsibility for the affirmations regarding article submission (eg, not under consideration by another journal), the integrity of the data presented, and the statements regarding compliance with institutional review board/Declaration of Helsinki requirements. Supporting information Disclosure form . ACR-77-953-s003.pdf (2.7MB, pdf) Appendix S1: CARRA guidance mental health in pediatric rheum draft v3 ACR-77-953-s002.pdf (893.1KB, pdf) Appendix S2: Supplementary Material for Review and Publication ACR-77-953-s001.docx (79KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We would like to thank all those who provided insight during the process of developing these guidance statements. We thank the following task force members: Anna Ramsey, Catherine Levalee, Alicia Hoffman, Samantha Mendoz, Tracy Van Ness, and Alicia Garceau. We also thank Emily von Scheven, who reviewed and provided feedback on the statements. We thank Michelle Adler and Jocelyn Zuckerman for formatting and reviewing this paper. Developed by the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance (CARRA) Mental Health Guidance Statement Task Force. The views expressed herein are those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance. Drs Cunningham and Danguecan contributed equally to this work. Drs Rubinstein and Knight contributed equally to this work. Additional supplementary information cited in this article can be found online in the Supporting Information section ( https://acrjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/acr.25519 ). Author disclosures and graphical abstract are available at https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/acr.25519 . REFERENCES 1. Reid M, Fabricius J, Danguecan A, et al. Anxiety and depression in childhood rheumatologic conditions: a topical review. Indian J Rheumatol 2021;16(3):304–310. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Quilter MC, Hiraki LT, Korczak DJ. Depressive and anxiety symptom prevalence in childhood‐onset systemic lupus erythematosus: a systematic review. Lupus 2019;28(7):878–887. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Fair DC, Rodriguez M, Knight AM, et al. Depression and anxiety in patients with juvenile idiopathic arthritis: current insights and impact on quality of life, a systematic review. Open Access Rheumatol 2019;11:237–252. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Treemarcki EB, Danguecan AN, Cunningham NR, et al. Mental health in pediatric rheumatology: an opportunity to improve outcomes. Rheum Dis Clin North Am 2022;48(1):67–90. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Davis AM, Graham TB, Zhu Y, et al. Depression and medication nonadherence in childhood‐onset systemic lupus erythematosus. Lupus 2018;27(9):1532–1541. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Donnelly C, Cunningham N, Jones JT, et al. Fatigue and depression predict reduced health‐related quality of life in childhood‐onset lupus. Lupus 2018;27(1):124–133. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Knight A, Weiss P, Morales K, et al. Depression and anxiety and their association with 