INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3562,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10766217','Evaluation of nurse practitioners'' roles and competencies among rheumatology nurses: Results of a national survey.','Wang Y, Chen Y, Liu H, Chen H, Liang Y.','2024','nprcs nprcs derangement nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs amenk unqualified nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs symposiums cooperating nprcs nprcs nprcs refreshment nprcs hamric nprcs nprcs nprcs nprcs embodies editsprings editsprings huan yanling yanling nprcs hxhl20039 e36842 yanwangbbao yanling huan hxlmliangyan jabi seleit badran gulliver beaudart biver bruyere musculo duffield collegian kapu kleinpell ambler raft raft ndosi primdahl holst ambra soubrier perrodeau comedra grønning rannestad skomsvoll reese staerker mulligen kuijper tara ndosi primdahl lopatina clercq shyu tsay ueng boville saran hamric kaohsiun kaohsiung','Abstract To investigate nurse practitioners’ roles and competencies among rheumatology nurses in China, an online, cross-sectional survey was conducted between July 7 and 14, 2020 among the national cooperation group of nursing experts on management of rheumatic and immune diseases. A total of 796 valid questionnaires were returned and participants’ mean total scores on the Nurse Practitioners’ Roles and Competencies Scale (NPRCS) was 2.51 (SD = 0.55), indicating a medium level. Medical assistance, leadership reform, and clinical research had low scores in the six dimensions of the NPRCS. Nurse practitioners’ roles and competencies in the area of rheumatology require improvement. Training should focus on medical assistance, leadership reform, and clinical research. The current study can provide a reference for an improved training framework of nursing practitioners in the rheumatology field. Keywords: advanced practice nursing, disease management, nurse practitioners, rheumatic diseases 1. Introduction Rheumatic diseases comprise heterogeneous disorders involving the musculoskeletal system, characterized by inflammation, degeneration, or metabolic derangement. Patients with rheumatic diseases may have decreased physical, emotional, and social functions due to disease activity, comorbidities, and treatment-related side effects [ 1 ] leading to significantly impaired health-related quality of life. [ 1 – 4 ] Rheumatic diseases are the second most common cause of disability and the second largest contributor to the burden of years lived with disability at the global level. [ 5 ] A national health service survey analysis (Center for Health Statistics and Information, 2015) ranked the prevalence of rheumatic diseases 3rd after circulatory and endocrine/metabolic diseases in China in 2013. A large population generates a high demand for cost-effective care of rheumatology patients and the incurable nature of most rheumatic diseases means that needs for disease management are long term. Standardized disease management is particularly important to ensure treatment compliance in patients with rheumatic diseases and to address the problems of physical, psychological, and social dysfunctions. [ 6 ] However, rheumatic disease management started late in China. Most clinical nurses in the rheumatology department currently conduct assessment, education, health promotion, and coordinated referrals under the guidance of rheumatologists. [ 6 ] The expansion and advancement of the roles and competencies of clinical nurses in rheumatology departments should take place to accommodate growing disease management needs of rheumatology patients. An advanced practice nurse (APN) is a registered nurse with extensive expertise, complex problem-solving, and extended clinical practice capabilities. Clinical nurse specialists, nurse practitioners (NPs), certified nurse midwives, nurse anesthetists, and case managers are all examples of APNs. [ 7 ] The role of NPs has arisen due to medical workforce shortages, increased demand for chronic disease care, and increased medical costs [ 8 ] and NPs have had a positive impact on medical quality and team efficiency, improving chronic disease health outcomes, reducing health costs, and promoting nursing disciplines. [ 9 ] NPs work independently with an emphasis on medical cooperation in hospitals, communities, and primary care centers. The scope of the NP’s workload includes disease assessment and diagnosis, disease disposal, health education, health promotion, coordinated referral, and cooperative care. Rheumatology nurses have been shown to have a positive impact on the quality of life and functional status of patients with rheumatic diseases via physiological, psychosocial, and social effects. [ 10 – 15 ] In addition, nurse-led care may reduce hospital stays and health care costs [ 16 – 19 ] and NPs may contribute to the reduction of congestion in tertiary hospitals by managing outpatients in community centers, reducing outpatient pressures, and overcrowding of urban hospitals. [ 20 ] Nurses have undertaken some advanced roles in rheumatic disease management, such as monitoring patients’ disease activity in lieu of a rheumatologist, providing patients with comorbidity screening and education, reviewing health conditions, performing wellness and lifestyle assessments, supporting standardized clinical documentation, and communicating with primary care providers. [ 21 ] Compared with other roles of APNs, the characteristics of NPs are closer to the needs of rheumatic disease management practice in China. Thus, the introduction of NPs may benefit the management of rheumatic diseases. Nevertheless, professional education in rheumatic disease nursing in China is still insufficient, not only less involved in school education, APN training is also in its infancy in China and lacks a clearly defined framework for NPs in rheumatic disease management. Therefore, the current study was undertaken to evaluate the roles and competencies of Chinese rheumatology NPs to provide for further development and training. 2. Methods 2.1. Study design A cross-sectional survey of rheumatology nurses from 126 hospitals in 68 cities in China was performed via a web-based approach between July 7 and July 14, 2020. The study complied with Strengthening the Reporting of Observational studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) guidelines. 2.2. Ethical considerations This study was conducted in accordance with the Helsinki declaration and ethical approval was obtained from the Medical Ethics Committee of the West China Hospital (ID: 2020466). All participants gave online informed consent prior to data collection. The informed consent form is set on the front page of the questionnaire, and participants must click “agree” to proceed to the next step. If they opt for “disagree,” they have the option to leave the questionnaire interface and quit at any time during the survey. 2.3. Participants Registered nurses working in the field of rheumatology were invited to participate in the online survey. The inclusion criteria were as follows: participants were >18 years of age; had a practice license and were registered; had worked in the field of rheumatology for at least 2 years; and voluntarily participated in this study. 2.4. Instrument The structured questionnaire sought demographic information: gender, age, and educational level; work status: title, years of work in the field of rheumatology, the grade of the hospital, specialized training, and participation in rheumatic disease management. Rheumatology NP roles and competencies were evaluated by the Nurse Practitioners’ Roles and Competencies Scale (NPRCS). The NPRCS was developed in 2006, [ 22 ] initially with 54 items and 9 dimensions. A 2016 revision resulted in 51 items in 6 dimensions, including professional competence (20 items), direct care (12 items), clinical research (5 items), practice guidance (4 items), medical assistance (7 items), and leadership reform (3 items). The professional competence dimension integrates 20 items from humanistic care, professional consultation, ethical decisions, communication and coordination, and leadership reform from the initial version. [ 23 ] Given that the positioning of the NP fits with the needs of chronic disease management, [ 24 ] we used the NPRCS to assess the role competence of rheumatology nurses. In addition, the NPRCS is suitable for evaluating the roles and competencies of NPs in Asian countries [ 23 ] and Traditional Chinese version was reported to be reliable and valid. [ 23 ] The traditional Chinese version of the NPRCS was used for the current study, transformed into simplified Chinese, and cultural adjustments incorporated. The Cronbach ɑ coefficient was 0.968. Scores between 1 and 4 could be given, ranging from “never involved,” “sometimes involved,” “often involved” to “always involved” in daily clinical practice. The mean score for each entry was considered the NPRCS score. 2.5. Data collection Data were collected by convenience sampling. The expected sample size by 5 to 20 times the number of questionnaire items would be 306 to 1224. This was an online study conducted through the national cooperation group of nursing experts on management of rheumatic and immune diseases. First, mature online questionnaire websites in China were assessed by researchers, electronic questionnaire links were created and tested repeatedly, and members of the organization were contacted to explain the purpose of the research and gain approval. They were sent the questionnaire link for distribution to other nurses in their department. Numbers of the same WeChat ID answer were limited for each question and a must-answer design implemented to avoid missing values. All data were checked by 2 researchers. Questionnaires with logic errors or with the same option for all answers were removed as part of the quality control process. 2.6. Data analysis Statistical analysis was performed by IBM SPSS 25.0 (Amenk, NY). Summary statistics for continuous variables are presented as mean and standard deviation (SD) or median and interquartile range (IQR) and categorical variables are reported as frequencies and percentages. Student t test and one-way analysis of variance were used for intergroup comparisons. The threshold for statistical significance was considered to be P < .05. 3. Results 3.1. Participant demographics In total, 849 questionnaires were returned, 53 unqualified questionnaires were removed and the validity rate was 93.76%. Participants were from 126 hospitals, ranging in age from 19 to 55 years (median = 30, IQR = 8), with employment histories of 1 to 32 years (median = 6, IQR = 7). The majority of participants were female (99.62%) and had a college education (76.26%). The results are shown in Table 1 . Table 1. Participants characteristics and univariate analysis of study variables. Variables Frequency (%) Median (IQR) NPRCS, mean (SD) Test statistics P value The grade of the hospital t = −0.654 * .513 Grade A tertiary hospital 766 (96.23) 2.51 (0.55) Others 30 (3.77) 2.58 (0.53) Educational level F = 2.009 † .135 Junior college or below 169 (21.23) 2.49 (0.59) college 607 (76.26) 2.52 (0.54) postgraduate 20 (2.51) 2.28 (0.32) Professional title F = 1.127 † .337 Assistant nurse 169 (21.23) 2.58 (0.61) Primary nurse 367 (46.11) 2.50 (0.55) Junior nurse 212 (26.63) 2.48 (0.52) Senior nurse 48 (6.03) 2.50 (0.47) Specialty training t = 4.352 * <.001 Yes 382 (47.99) 2.60 (0.55) No 414 (52.01) 2.43 (0.54) Had carried out rheumatic disease management t = 4.454 * <.001 Yes 473 (59.42) 2.58 (0.57) No 323 (40.58) 2.41 (0.51) Had participated in rheumatic disease management t = 3.470 * .001 Yes 87 (10.93) 2.70 (0.59) No 709 (89.07) 2.49 (0.54) Age(years) 30 (8) 2.51 (0.55) ρ = −0.040 ‡ .257 Years of employment 6 (7) 2.51 (0.55) ρ = −0.056 ‡ .114 Open in a new tab IQR = interquartile range, NPRCS = Nurse Practitioners’ Roles and Competencies Scale, SD = standard deviation. * Statistic based on independent samples t test ( t value). † Statistic based on one-way between-groups analysis of variance ( F value). ‡ Statistic based on Spearman correlation ( ρ value). 3.2. Nurse practitioners’ roles and competencies among rheumatology nurses The mean NPRCS score was 2.51 (SD = 0.55) out of a possible maximum of 4, showing a medium level of competency. The mean ranking scores of the 6 dimensions were as follows: practice guidance (2.89, SD = 0.72), professional ability (2.73, SD = 0.63), direct care (2.61, SD = 0.63), medical assistance (2.10, SD = 0.61), leadership reform (2.03.SD = 0.83), and clinical research (1.91, SD = 0.65). No statistically significant differences in NPRCS scores relating to the grade of the hospital, educational level and professional title, or ages and working years were found ( P > .05). Participants with specialized training ( P < .001), whose department accommodated rheumatic disease management ( P < .001) and who participated in rheumatic disease management ( P = .002), had higher NPRCS scores than those who did not fulfill these criteria. Items giving the bottom 10 NPRCS scores came from the following dimensions: medical assistance (3 items), clinical research (3 items), leadership reform (3 items), and professional ability (1 item; Table 2 ). Table 2. The bottom 10 items in the Nurse Practitioners’ Roles and Competencies Scale. Ranking Dimensions Items Scores, mean (SD) 1 Medical assistance Assisting in prescribing medication within the permitted scope of nursing practice 1.43 (0.80) 2 Medical assistance Assisting in prescribing tests and examinations 1.49 (0.80) 3 Clinical research Independently executing a research project 1.60 (0.81) 4 Clinical research Oral presentation and publishing articles via posters in academic symposiums 1.72 (0.83) 5 Clinical research Cooperating with others to execute research projects 1.83 (0.83) 6 Leadership and reform Participating in promotion of association-related affairs 1.96 (0.92) 7 Leadership and reform Actively participating in medical prevention and voluntary medical consultation activities for various cultures and populations 2.01 (0.94) 8 Medical assistance Assisting in prescribing oxygen therapy 2.01 (1.00) 9 Leadership and reform Joining relevant professional associations 2.11 (0.88) 10 Professionalism Integrating patients’ test results with clinical ones and participating in medical and ethical decision-making 2.12 (0.97) Open in a new tab 4. Discussion The mean NPRCS score was 2.51 (SD = 0.55) out of a possible maximum of 4, indicating scope for further development of rheumatology NP roles and competencies. The grade of the hospital, nurses’ educational level, professional title, age, and working years were not associated with NPRCS scores. However, nurses who had received specialized training, whose department accommodated rheumatic disease management, or who had participated in rheumatic disease management had higher NPRCS scores. A possible explanation for these findings is that nurses in receipt of specialized training may be more experienced in the theory and practice of rheumatic disease management. In addition, rheumatic disease management required nurses to independently perform disease activity assessments, health education, psychological counseling, and implementation of medical assistance. These activities impose higher demands for practical clinical knowledge/skills, communication/coordination, continuation of training, knowledge refreshment, and the requirement to extend knowledge and conduct scientific research. Thus, systematic training and clinical practice experience are likely to improve rheumatology NP roles and competencies. Training for rheumatic disease management should include both theoretical and practical aspects. Dimensions with higher NPRCS scores included practical guidance, professional competence, and direct care, consistent with the research findings of Chinese nurse specialists. [ 25 ] Practical guidance refers to the NPs’ educational guidance to medical teams, nursing students, patients, and their families. [ 23 ] The role of educator is the most important NP function and frequently involved in clinical practice. The professional competence dimension includes humanistic care, ethical decisions, communication, and coordination required in clinical practice, [ 23 ] similar to the occupational competency framework of clinical nurse specialists. [ 26 ] Humanistic care is the core of nursing practice and ethics inform nursing behavior while communication and coordination are central to nursing. Direct care includes evaluation of patients, formulation of care plans, implementation of patient care, observation and recording of changes in conditions, and active communication with the patients. Hamric and Hanson [ 27 ] proposed that direct care is central to an APN’s role. Clinical care is also vital. [ 28 , 29 ] Rheumatic disease management requires nurses to carry out risk assessments, use communication skills to implement health education, and provide humanistic care and optimal management plans. Therefore, practice guidance, humanistic care, ethical decision-making, communication, coordination, and clinical care are vital and should be the focus of training and promotion. Dimensions with lower NPRCS scores were medical assistance, leadership reform, and clinical research, consistent with Lin’s study. [ 30 ] Medical assistance refers to the completion of medical procedures under physician guidance, including assistance with clear diagnosis, invasive examination, treatment and disposal, drug administration, and condition communication. [ 23 ] Among the 10 items with the lowest NPRCS scores, 3 were from the medical assistance dimension: “prescribed within the scope of the nursing practice license,” “assist in opening the test list and checklist,” and “assist in prescribing oxygen therapy.” National policy may have an impact on these scores since Chinese law demands that only registered doctors prescribe medicine and perform examinations. APNs in developed countries, such as Great Britain and the United States, have prescription rights but Master’s or Doctoral degrees are required. [ 29 ] However, Chinese rheumatic disease management nurses are involved in patient referral and medical coordination and assist doctors in completing appointments, examinations, and invasive operations. Legislation has recently been passed in a Chinese city enabling nurse specialists to issue prescriptions, a positive move for the extension of nurses’ roles. Nurses with specialist certificates may issue examination applications, treatment applications, and topical drugs and execute other professional activities in specialist nursing clinics or community health service institutions. [ 31 ] Therefore, the training of rheumatology nurses should be tailored to their knowledge and practical ability. Leadership reform refers to leadership roles in clinical practice, professional development, and policy making. [ 23 ] All 3 items belonging to this dimension are within the bottom 10 of the NPRCS scores, including “active participation in medical prevention and voluntary medical counseling activities for different cultures and ethnicities,” “participation in the promotion of medical-related associations,” and “participation in related professional associations.” Clinical and professional leadership is a core competency of the APN. [ 27 ] Leadership reform scores were low in the current study and it was necessary to include relevant contents and assessment items in the NP training framework of rheumatic disease management. Clinical research refers to the provision of evidence-based clinical care and participation in scientific research and publication findings. [ 23 ] The clinical research dimension had the lowest score and 3 of its 5 items were ranked in the bottom 10 of the NPRCS scores, including “oral speech and published articles through posters at academic seminars,” “executive research project independently,” and “research project in cooperation with others.” This finding may be explained by low educational levels among rheumatology nurses. Only 2.51% of the current participants had a Master’s degree or above. An investigation of scientific research capabilities among 1199 specialized nurses in China found those of clinical specialized nurses to be at a lower level. [ 32 ] Such findings illustrate the indispensable nature of the clinical research competency components of the NP training framework in rheumatic disease management. Rheumatology is a relatively young discipline in China and disease management has recent origins. Current rheumatic disease management is mainly based on experience summaries. Therefore, the need to train NPs in rheumatic disease management provision, development, and research is urgent. More attention should be given to medical assistance, leadership reform, and clinical research in rheumatic disease management in the development of the rheumatology NP training curriculum. There are several limitations to the current study. First, the data resulted from self-assessment and may lack objectivity. Second, selection bias may have been introduced by the recruitment of participants via a web-based approach. Third, the cross-sectional nature means that only the current status of the role and competence of Chinese rheumatology NPs can be evaluated and causal relationships cannot be inferred. Therefore, more research with large samples is needed to prospectively explore the factors associated with role improvement in rheumatology nurses. 5. Conclusions The development of NPs for rheumatic disease management has practical significance in view of the growing care needs of patients with rheumatic diseases and the shortage of rheumatologists. The NPs’ roles and competencies of rheumatology nurses require improvement via training and extension of nurses’ roles. A curriculum for standard training that embodies factors related to NPs’ roles and competencies should be developed. Acknowledgments The authors acknowledge National cooperation group of nursing experts on management of rheumatic and immune diseases for support and assistance in data collection. The authors would like to acknowledge the Department of Clinical Research Management of West China Hospital for support and assistance in data analysis. The authors would like to thank all respondents in this study. The authors would like to express their gratitude to EditSprings ( https://www.editsprings.cn ) for the expert linguistic services provided. Author contributions Conceptualization: Ying Wang, Hong Chen. Investigation: Ying Wang, Yan Liang. Validation: Ying Wang. Writing – original draft: Ying Wang. Writing – review & editing: Ying Wang, Huan Liu, Hong Chen. Data curation: Yanling Chen. Formal analysis: Yanling Chen. Methodology: Hong Chen. Abbreviations: APN advanced practice nurse IQR interquartile range NPRCS Nurse Practitioners’ Roles and Competencies Scale NPs nurse practitioners SD standard deviation STROBE Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology. The current status of the nurse practitioners’ roles and competencies of rheumatology nurses in China is presented. The results of this study can provide reference for an improved training framework of nursing practitioners in the rheumatology field. This study is supported by the West China Hospital, Sichuan University under West China Nursing Discipline Development Special Fund Project, Sichuan University (number HXHL20039). The authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. How to cite this article: Wang Y, Chen Y, Liu H, Chen H, Liang Y. Evaluation of nurse practitioners’ roles and competencies among rheumatology nurses: Results of a national survey. Medicine 2024;103:1(e36842). Contributor Information Ying Wang, Email: yanwangbbao@163.com. Yanling Chen, Email: 1366109878@qq.com. Huan Liu, Email: 907857593@qq.com. Yan Liang, Email: hxlmliangyan@163.com. References [1]. Al-Jabi SW, Seleit DI, Badran A, et al. Impact of socio-demographic and clinical characteristics on functional disability and health-related quality of life in patients with rheumatoid arthritis: a cross-sectional study from Palestine. Health Qual Life Out. 2021;19:241. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Ward MM. Outcome measurement: health status and quality of life. Curr Opin Rheumatol. 2004;16:96–101. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Russell AS, Gulliver WP, Irvine EJ, et al. Quality of life in patients with immune-mediated inflammatory diseases. J Rheumatol Suppl. 2011;88:7–19. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Beaudart C, Biver E, Bruyère O, et al. Quality of life assessment in musculo-skeletal health. Aging Clin Exp Res. 2018;30:413–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Vos T, Flaxman AD, Naghavi M, et al. Years lived with disability (YLDs) for 1160 sequelae of 289 diseases and injuries 1990-2010: a systematic analysis for the global burden of disease study 2010. Lancet. 2012;380:2163–96. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Yang H, Zhao Y, Liu Y. Chronic disease management of rheumatic diseases, are you ready? Chin J Intern Med. 2017;56:633–4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [7]. Duffield C, Gardner G, Chang AM, et al. Advanced nursing practice: a global perspective. Collegian. 2009;16:55–62. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [8]. Parker JM, Hill MN. A review of advanced practice nursing in the United States, Canada, Australia and Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (SAR), China. Int J Nurs Sci. 2017;4:196–204. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [9]. Kapu AN, Kleinpell R. Developing nurse practitioner associated metrics for outcomes assessment. J Am Assoc Nurse Pract. 2013;25:289–96. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ G