ng information. HEX-27-e14049-s001.docx (45.3KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to privacy or ethical restrictions.',44,'2025-10-15 08:39:08','2025-10-15 08:39:08');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3591,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4954489','Attitudes and perceptions of rheumatologists regarding consultation skills training for specialist trainees: a qualitative study.','Sandhu RS, Ong BN, Cooper V, Hassell AB.','2010','deanery videotape bedrock oversubscribed deaneries yorkshire mrec rss taped videotaping audiotapes unconnected intimidated honed jeopardised abysmally educationalist dogma ironic criticised resorting apprehensive videotapes videotaping motivator videotaping videotape shortcoming buyck howells judgemental videotape rss klocke snowden kurtz radcliffe madray ardolino willms amiel ungar alperin norcini fortna wragg lyle mrcgp foulkes neighbour campion ziebland dipex qsr pty novack drossman lipkin klass ecfmg hulsman winnubst bensing fallowfield farewell solis trapala rediscovering taped fallowfield lipkin jrcptb buyck howells','Abstract Within rheumatology specialty training, direct observation and formal assessment of consultation skills rarely occur. This study explored the attitudes and perceptions of rheumatology specialist registrars (SpRs) and consultants regarding consultation skills training and potential barriers to its successful implementation in the context of specialist training. Semi-structured interviews with rheumatology consultants and focus groups with rheumatology SpRs were conducted in four UK deanery regions. All participants value consultation skills training and believe it requires observation of trainees consulting. The skills of consultant trainers in providing feedback on consulting skills are sometimes sub-optimal. Direct, real time observation of trainees is difficult and happens infrequently. Recording consultations is a potentially attractive alternative. Important issues regarding the successful implementation of videotaped consultations include time constraints, consistency in the assessment of consultation skills, and expertise in providing constructive feedback that is individualised and tailored to the trainee''s learning needs. Key Words: assessment, communication, consultation, education, videotape Introduction Highly developed consultation skills are essential for rheumatologists. Competence in consultation incorporates the complex interaction of specific skills including not only communication skills, but also knowledge, problem solving and physical examination skills. 1 Communication and consultation skills are now taught widely in UK medical schools and are core abilities required by the General Medical Council. 2 There are strong arguments for continuing consultation skills training and assessment at a postgraduate level in rheumatology and in other specialties. Effective consultations within rheumatology depend upon the integration of skills and knowledge developed during postgraduate training. The consultation is the bedrock of practice in medicine. Yet often the development of consultation skills in higher medical training is taken for granted rather than consciously fostered. Moreover, studies have demonstrated that communication skills acquired by undergraduates decline soon after training. 3 Clinical experience alone has been shown not to correlate with communication skills proficiency. 4 Historically, as with other medical subspecialties, there has been no culture of direct observation of consultation skills within rheumatology. The mini-Clinical Evaluation Exercise (mini-CEX) is one step towards direct observation of practice. 5 , 6 However, direct observation involves both the consultant and the trainee doctor seeing one patient at the same time, which can slow the running of often oversubscribed clinics. This can have a negative impact on service delivery and on the timeliness of feedback. The use of videotaped consultations is an integral part of both training and assessment of general practice (GP) trainees in the UK. 7 There is evidence that assessment of videotaped consultation skills is reliable and valid. 8 However, little is known about the acceptability or feasibility of such assessments in hospital specialist training. Given the relative paucity of consultation skills training in rheumatology, the objective of this study was to explore the attitudes to consultation skills training of rheumatology trainees and their consultant trainers in four different UK deaneries. At the same time, participants’ views on the potential for videotaped consultations in this context were explored. Methods Research method Focus groups were held with rheumatology SpRs in four UK regions: the West Midlands, North West, Yorkshire and Northern. 9 Consultants participated in individual semi-structured interviews. Focus groups were not considered feasible because of logistical considerations. Recruitment An invitation letter and an information sheet were emailed to all rheumatology SpRs in a region via a local collaborating rheumatologist. A reminder email was sent out two weeks after the original email. Focus groups took place on SpR regional training in order to maximise recruitment. Consultant participants were invited to participate by email. A purposive sample of one senior (.10 years’ experience) and one junior (,5 years’ experience) consultant was selected for interview in each region. Selection was after discussion with the local collaborating physician. Multi-centre Research Ethics Committee (MREC) approval was given for the study. Data collection One focus group and two consultant interviews were conducted in each of the four regions. All focus groups and individual interviews were semi-structured, facilitated by one investigator (RSS), audio-taped and transcribed verbatim. Participants were asked to describe their experiences in consultation skills training and assessment throughout their career, beginning from medical school. Consultants were also asked about their experience in teaching and assessing the consultation skills of their trainees. All participants were asked about the need for consultation skills training in the context of specialist training. The acceptability of videotaping consultations in rheumatology outpatient clinics was also explored. Data analysis Audiotapes were listened to following the interviews (both focus groups and individual interviews) and prior to further interviews. In subsequent interviews, the researcher was therefore able to explore any new themes that had arisen and thus provide richer interpretive data. The verbatim transcripts served as the raw data to be analysed and thematic analysis was used. 10 Coding was carried out with the aid of N-vivo (version 2) data management software. 11 The analysis and corresponding raw data were then presented to the co-authors of this study who gave their input into the interpretation of themes. This provided further development of the analysis and conceptual arguments. An independent, experienced qualitative researcher unconnected with the study coded one of the focus group transcripts. This exercise revealed a close agreement between coders. Results Participants Participant data for the SpR focus groups and consultant interviews are illustrated in Tables 1 and 2 respectively. Participants are referred to using the coding scheme: ‘Con’ consultant, ‘SpR’ specialist registrar, followed by an assigned identity number. Table 1. Participant demographic data for specialist registrar (SpR) focus groups. Open in a new tab Table 2. Participant data for consultant interviews. Open in a new tab Participants’ experience of consultation skills training Three of the 24 SpRs and two senior consultants did not recall any consultation skills training at medical school. Both consultants qualified in the 1970s. Two of the three SpRs had trained in India. The remaining SpR was in the final year of training. For those who received it, general practice training had provided the only systematic communication skills teaching. None of the consultants recalled having any postgraduate training or assessment in communication or consultation skills. Consultants recalled emphasis on other aspects of medical training: I don''t think there was much emphasis on communication. I think the emphasis was more on your knowledge, attaining knowledge and applying your knowledge and learning to be a doctor, a good doctor, so by knowing your stuff, you were a good doctor. (Con 3) In the postgraduate setting, both rheumatology consultants and SpRs felt that they had developed their consultation skills by observation of colleagues and modelling aspects of their behaviour. All participants agreed that formal consultation skills training is not currently taking place. All participants discussed the introduction of the mini-CEX as a method of observation and assessment of SpRs in the consultation. Consultants on the whole viewed it as useful but had concerns about it being resource intensive and difficult to manage within a busy clinical service. They also had concerns about how accurately they were assessing the true performance of their SpRs: You know, you''ve got to try and concentrate. They (the SpR) are trying to be on their best behaviour and slightly intimidated by your presence. The patient, especially if it was your patient, you wouldn''t know, they might be turning to you and all these sort of things that you might influence. (Con 6) For their part, SpRs felt that consultants had little time to provide constructive feedback during the mini-CEX as it often took place in the middle of a busy clinic. SpRs perceived variation in the quality of the feedback from different consultants. The perceived need for consultation skills training in the context of higher medical training All consultants reported that SpRs were rarely observed in consultation and a more objective assessment of their consultation skills was required. Three of the eight consultants (two junior and one senior) debated the need for further consultation skills training at SpR level, because they perceived the majority of SpRs to be consulting to an acceptable standard: I’m not sure (about need for training). You would hope that they would have had the training earlier on in the career. You''d hope that by the time they got to SpR level, that their consultation skills are good, or honed. So, I seem to be making quite a lot of assumptions there really…. I suppose we should really assess what consultation skills are like in our SpRs and if they are not good, then there is a need for training. (Con 1) Five of the eight consultants did feel the need for structured training specifically at SpR level. Reasons given included shorter training periods for SpRs and a reduction in working hours both of which, it was felt, jeopardised consultation skills development in an apprenticeship model of learning. All SpRs considered that there is a need for more structured training in consultation skills within higher medical training. They reported the desire to develop both communication and other (eg problem solving and time management) consultation skills. They felt that they are rarely observed in consultation and expressed some doubts about self-assessment of their performance: If you go through the entire lot of your training thinking that you''re doing a job well and in actual fact you''re doing it abysmally and there''s no-one around to point it out because no-one''s ever watched you. (SpR 1a) Observation and feedback on consultation skills were considered important. SpRs reported that they had poor access to consultants in the outpatient clinics because of the demands placed on them by medical students and more junior colleagues. Priorities in implementing consultation skills training within higher medical training Protected time was considered an essential element to the successful implementation of training. This issue was raised in all focus groups and interviews. Time would be required in order to observe SpRs in consultation and provide good quality feedback. SpRs felt that consultation skills training should be individualised in order to address their learning needs. In this context, they believed that it was important to have a rheumatologist experienced in giving feedback. All consultants felt confident in their ability to provide feedback in a formative setting. This was despite having little formal consultation skills training themselves. Some of the consultants felt that they were better suited to provide feedback than other communication/consultation skills teachers who were ‘non-rheumatologists’: Yeah, I wouldn''t trust an educationalist…. You know, there would be some dogma that went with it, that just wouldn''t function and, you know, they [SpRs] need to be assessed by people who know what the consultation is about because I think I now understand pretty well what I’m doing in clinic. (Con 6) SpRs perceived a lack of consensus on what constitutes a ‘good consultation’. Consultants’ feedback emphasised aspects of the consultation that differed from the SpRs’ expectations. This impacted on the acceptability of the feedback provided: But then it was ironic… I was explaining this to the patient and I was asking her ‘what do you think is wrong and what''s your opinion of the disease’, and doing all these nice things and I got heavily criticised for resorting to generic communication things and not being more medical and rheumatological about it. (SpR 2a) In addition to the content of the feedback, the process by which it is delivered was also considered important. It was evident from the SpR comments that a hierarchical relationship exists between themselves and their consultant colleagues. SpRs therefore felt that the feedback they received was not always a two-way process: But then I know better to disagree with [the consultant] who is assessing you, do you know what I mean? So I felt that [the feedback] was interesting but I would like to have taken that further. But I didn''t really get the chance. It''s like, ‘Here''s your bit of paper, I’m going now’. (SpR 3d) Both consultants and SpRs believed it was important to give balanced and constructive feedback. Attitudes to videotaped consultations in the workplace When questioned about the use of videotaped consultations with real patients in the workplace, SpRs perceived this method of training to have potential although they were apprehensive about personal involvement and felt it should be confined to formative assessment. Practical challenges were identified, including the availability of recording equipment, clear guidance on obtaining patient consent and storage of videotapes. Seven of the eight consultants favoured videotaping consultations, believing it to be more practical and effective than direct observation: I think that a great motivator for change is to see yourself do it. Mini-CEX doesn''t allow you to do that, you just hear what somebody thinks and to be honest, I don''t think that''s as persuasive as you seeing yourself. (Con 4) Most of the consultants felt videotaping consultations enabled more efficient use of time by avoiding the need for them to sit in on their trainee''s consultations. Both consultants and SpRs believed that acceptability of videotape would be enhanced by provision of objective evidence of its efficacy in comparison with other methods of learning consultation skills. Both groups also perceived a changing attitude to postgraduate training and assessment in that structured training and objective assessment have become the requisite. Discussion This study identifies an evolving culture among rheumatology trainers and trainees regarding consultation skills training and assessment. There is a growing perception of a need to observe trainee rheumatologists consulting in order to provide constructive, robust feedback. Videotaped consultations are felt to offer potential in this context although a number of challenges to their successful implementation in rheumatology training were identified. The study highlights the variable experience of trainee doctors in consultation skills training, also noted in other studies. 12 Most SpRs who had not received structured training as undergraduates, trained abroad. It cannot be assumed that all trainees have had training in consultation skills before entering specialist training. 13 There is considerable evidence that consultation skills training is effective at modifying doctors’ behaviour and that postgraduate doctors retain these skills post-training and develop them further with time. 14 , 15 Despite this, in contrast with general practice, there is little consultation skills training in rheumatology specialist training or, indeed, in the other medical subspecialties. 16 A review of the literature shows that, in the secondary care setting, consultation skills training has been embraced by oncology but by few other specialties. 17 It could be argued that there is little evidence to suggest poor performance in consultation skills of physicians and SpRs. However, consultations are rarely observed. Studies suggest that many patient complaints are a result of poor communication. 18 Moreover, evidence indicates that most clinicians can benefit from consultation skills development. 14 Both consultants and SpRs expressed concerns regarding the effectiveness of the mini-CEX, in which assessors make global judgements in a number of areas that define clinical competence. A major shortcoming identified by all is that it is often not given the time required because of pressures of service delivery. Additionally, the scoring of a mini-CEX is against broad areas, which can pose difficulties in providing feedback in an area in which the consultants may have had little formal training themselves. 19 Our results show that SpRs and consultants frequently had different ideas about the important aspects of the consultation, as reported by Buyck and Lang. 20 Poorly standardised and unreliable feedback can have a negative impact on learning and influence trainees’ attitudes to the usefulness of such training and assessment. It is interesting that, whereas consultant participants felt confident in their ability to provide feedback on consultations, trainees perceived this to be a specific skill not possessed by all rheumatologists. Howells, Davis and Silverman outline the fundamental principles of feedback, including clarity of purpose, allowing self-assessment of the consultation by the learner first, being behaviour specific rather than judgemental, being concise and balancing reinforcing statements with corrective ones. 21 In conclusion, consultation skills training is deemed important by rheumatology SpRs and consultants. It is felt to be most effective when it involves direct observation of practice, and this is supported by the literature. Currently, such training happens infrequently. The use of videotape is viewed as having potential as a pragmatic way to observe and provide feedback upon consultations in the context of a busy clinical environment. Areas of future research include the characterisation of those consultation skills which are key in the context of rheumatology specialist training and the development of simple tools for facilitating effective provision of feedback on consultation skills. Funding RSS is supported by an Arthritis Research Campaign Educational Research Fellowship (number: 17136). Acknowledgements We would like to thank the following who helped organise interviews and focus groups in each of the four regions in which this study was conducted: Drs AK Brown, A Gough, W Dixon, R Klocke, N Snowden, D Walker and Mrs AM Smith. We also thank all rheumatology consultants and SpRs who participated in the interviews and focus groups. References 1. Kurtz S, Silverman J, Draper J. Teaching and learning communication skills in medicine. 2nd edn. Oxford: Radcliffe; 2005. [ Google Scholar ] 2. General Medical Council Tomorrow''s doctors. London: GMC, 2003 [ Google Scholar ] 3. Pfeiffer C, Madray H, Ardolino A, Willms J.The rise and fall of students’ skill in obtaining a medical history. Med Educ 1998;32:283–8 10.1046/j.1365-2923.1998.00222.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Amiel G, Ungar L, Alperin M, et al. Ability of primary care physician''s to break bad news: a performance based assessment of an educational intervention. Patient Educ Couns 2006;60:10–5 10.1016/j.pec.2005.04.013 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Norcini JJ, Blank LL, Duffy FD, Fortna GS.The mini-CEX: a method for assessing clinical skills. Ann Intern Med 2003;138:476–81 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Wragg A, Wade W, Fuller G, Cowan G, Mills P.Assessing the performance of specialist registrars. Clin Med 2003;3:131–4 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Lyle P.The video component of the MRCGP examination. Br J Gen Pract 2003;53:243. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Tate P, Foulkes J, Neighbour R, Campion P, Field S.Assessing physicians’ interpersonal skills via videotaped encounters: a new approach for the Royal College of General Practitioners Membership examination. J Health Comm 1999;4:143–52 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Krueger RA, Casey MA. Focus groups – a practical guide for applied research. 3rd edn. Thousand Oaks, CA: Sage; 2000. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Ziebland S, McPherson A.Making sense of qualitative data analysis: an introduction with illustrations from DIPEx (personal experiences of health and illness). Med Educ 2006;40:405–14 10.1111/j.1365-2929.2006.02467.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Melbourne: QSR International Pty Ltd; 2002. N-vivo 2 qualitative data analysis programme. [ Google Scholar ] 12. Novack DH, Volk G, Drossman DA, Lipkin M., Jr.Medical interviewing and interpersonal skills teaching in US medical schools. Progress, problems, and promise. JAMA 1993;269:2101–5 [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Ben D, Klass DJ, Boulet J, et al. The performance of foreign medical graduates on the National Board of Medical Examiners NBME standardized patient examination prototype: a collaborative study of the NBME and the Educational Commission for Foreign Medical Graduates ECFMG. Med Educ 1999;33:439–46 10.1046/j.1365-2923.1999.00368.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Hulsman RL, Ros WJ, Winnubst JA, Bensing JM.Teaching clinically experienced physicians communication skills: a review of evaluation studies. Med Educ 1999;33:655–68 10.1046/j.1365-2923.1999.00519.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Fallowfield L, Jenkins V, Farewell V, Solis-Trapala I.Enduring impact of communication skills training: results of a 12-month follow-up. Br J Cancer 2003;89:1445–9 10.1038/sj.bjc.6601309 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Fuller G, Smith P.Rediscovering the wheel – teaching communication skills using video taped clinical consultations in specialist training. Clin Med 2001;1:203–4 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Fallowfield L, Lipkin M, Hall A.Teaching senior oncologists communication skills: results from phase I of a comprehensive longitudinal program in the United Kingdom. J Clin Oncol 1998;16:1961–8 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Levinson W.Physician-patient communication. A key to malpractice prevention. JAMA 1994;272:1619–20 [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Joint Royal Colleges of Physicians Training Board RCP mini clinical evaluation exercise. London: JRCPTB, 2007 [ Google Scholar ] 20. Buyck D, Lang F.Teaching medical communication skills: a call for greater uniformity. Fam Med 2002;34:337–43 [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Howells RJ, Davies HA, Silverman JD.Teaching and learning consultation skills for paediatric practice. Arch Dis Child 2006;91:367–70 10.1136/adc.2005.073775 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',37,'2025-10-15 08:39:21','2025-10-15 08:39:21');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3592,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11427138','The inequity of global healthcare in pediatric rheumatology.','Vilaiyuk S, Hadef D, Hamdi W, Scott C, Slamang W, Foster HE, Lewandowski LB.','2024','futhermore hrics mrics lrics oligoarthritis oligoarthritis lric lrics mrics hrics hrics lrics mrics hrics lric lrics climates lric precedence talented jamless paflar paflar aplar aplar lrics mrics fewest hrics orthopedists rightpath pmskglobal teleteaching paflar aplar lrics mrics wealthier lrics mrics msus lrics lrics mrlcs laos macroeconomic tending unaffordability hrics laos lrics hrics nonexistent gtf paflar aplar gtf emlc lrics eml emlc nlem eml emlc nlem gtf eml eml lrics mrics jamless sjia aplar lrics mrics hrics unaffordability lrics lrics lrics fewest manar fmt rnu aplar teleteaching paflar paflar paflar aplar aplar aplar aplar aplar lrics mrics msus lrics pmmonline pres2023 kbe lrics mrics hrics hrics hrics hrics lrics printo lrics hrics printo printo hrics epoca epoca comprehending hrics hrics lrics upskill lrics mrics teleteaching lrics mrics hrics lrics mrics lrics mrics lrics mrics printo lrics mrics interconnect lrics gtf hrics mrics lrics nlem ramathibodi mahidol ramathibodi siriraj servant csmbs ucs csmbs ucs nlem newness hitap hitap sjia sjia nlem sjia sjia ucs csmbs sjia sjia nelm sjia ucs lrics mrics hrics lrics mrics botucatu botucatu gtf aldridge gtf sirirat charuvanij siriraj mahidol niams dreinhofer sebbag felten sagez s243 kyu pinho atijosan simms kuper diepeveen rischewski kuper atijosan petty southwood consolaro ravelli consolaro giancane alongi fujikawa okuni vilaiyuk soponkanaporn jaovisidha kunjir venugopalan e70625 tiderius broadaway ponder pelajo prahalad pineles kamphuis denardo hiraki kurahara grandinetti ardoin schanberg tektonidou pattaragarn sumboonnanonda parichatikanond s232 samanta nandi mondal balkaran ramcharan trinidadian tikly navarra jsle arfaj balla dalaan sule fivush nee osorio hagelberg bargman longterm ravelli ruperto schanberg thielman moosa irastorza khamashta devidayal hiraki benseler tyrrell ruperto gastaldi ravelli buratti bandeira buratti bartoli descloux durieu cochat abdelrahman beresford mayouf jkk wky hiraki benseler tyrrell zuhlke artim esen cene fatemi matinfar smiley phatak jongvilaikasem dissaneewate abdwani abdalla zakwani hadef slimani arthrite idiopathique avancees defis batna sculley migowa hadef hamdi henrickson migowa bernatsky ngugi iceberg slamang jamless cradle humankind katso baah bilsborrow pelaez ballestas estel sawhney schanberg thielman tiziani migowa bernatsky ngugi nganga makhlouf nessib ferjani mayouf mutairi bouayed farman teleteaching explor tiderius chausset belot jir mayouf hashad khawaja tzaribachev benseler tyrrell freychet shoop worrall moull chausset echaubard rapley mcerlane tekin celikel barnabe benseler hadef slimani khamari batna srinivasalu riebschleger henrickson alkwai alshammari abdwani jrd rosina rebollo gimenez consolaro migowa hamdi hashad paflar pietzonka biotherapeutics arkachaisri tangcheewinsirikul orlewska ancuta anic cee sr1 peterman vashi govan asmamaw bezie asfaw eml slamang onel oligoarthritis bilsborrow pelaez ballestas estel putrik ramiro kvien henrickson nganga kawishe ngugi luchters kiswahili patwardhan henrickson laskosz vojinovic jandial rapley jandial charani abimbola e0000583 sadun belot goulart mfg agf farhat ringold beukelman kittiratchakool kulpokin chanjam e037588 pacharapakornpong vallibhakara lerkvaleekul','Abstract In pediatric rheumatology, global health inequity relates to the uneven distribution of healthcare resources, accessibility, and health outcomes among children with rheumatic conditions across various countries, regions, and socioeconomic groups. This inequity can manifest in various ways. This review article provides an overview of common rheumatic diseases, such as juvenile idiopathic arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus, which significantly contribute to and are affected by disparities in global healthcare. Subsequently, we delve into the inequalities in accessing patient care, encompassing issues related to diagnosis and treatment. Additionally, we address challenges in educational advancement and identify research gaps within the field of pediatric rheumatology. We also reveal successful global collaborations, such as a Global Task Force for Pediatric Musculoskeletal Health and special working groups among international organizations, aimed at bridging the disparities gap. Through these efforts, we try to enhance understanding, cooperation, and resource allocation to ensure equal access to quality care worldwide for children with rheumatic conditions. Futhermore, we present a case study from Thailand, highlighting their successful initiatives in developing pediatric rheumatology within their healthcare system. Introduction Significant advancements have been made in the diagnosis and therapy approaches for children with musculoskeletal (MSK) conditions. However, it is crucial to recognize that a substantial number of children in worldwide are still deprived of these benefits [ 1 ]. One of the United Nations’ sustainable development goals is promoting well-being and people’s abilities, regardless of socioeconomic status, gender, ethnic background, or racial identity. This important principle should be applied to early childhood, children, youth, and elderly people. In order to achieve the goals of enhancing living standards, the world needs novel technologies, including medicines and diagnostics, which can be utilized in high-resource income (HRICs) middle-resource income countries (MRICs) and low-resource income countries (LRICs) [ 2 ]. The need to address global healthcare inequities, specifically in relation to MSK diseases, is imperative, given the acknowledgment that access to healthcare is a fundamental human right and ethical responsibility. This urgency arises from estimated global burden of disease: approximately one in three individuals experiences a chronic MSK condition, resulting in various adverse consequences such as pain, poor quality of life, mental health disorders, disability, and mortality [ 3 – 6 ]. MSK conditions are non-communicable, but the relative burden has increased over the past several years from 2.5% in 2004 to 5.9% in 2016 [ 7 , 8 ]. Increases in these conditions have been found in low-income countries [ 9 ] due to delays in diagnosis and appropriate treatment, stemming from limited access to healthcare providers, poor education, and socioeconomic issues. Furthermore, early access to therapy can prevent damage in a growing child, adding to the imperative to diagnoses and treat these patients. Previous studies revealed that the community-wide burden of pediatric MSK conditions was higher than hospital-based or clinic-based assessment [ 10 , 11 ]. MSK conditions carry economic impact, such as reduced productive years of work from illness, the impact on caregivers and families due to loss of income, and increased healthcare costs, impeding global economic progression. Children with disabilities have limited access to educational options, which subsequently diminishes their quality of life and prospects for work as adults [ 12 ]. Overview of Pediatric Rheumatic Diseases The most common chronic inflammatory MSK conditions in children are juvenile idiopathic arthritis and juvenile systemic lupus erythematosus; these are summarized below and have significant impact and consequences due to inequities in global healthcare. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA) Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA) is the most common chronic arthritis in children. It occurs in children younger than 16 years, and arthritis has to persist for more than six weeks. According to the International League of Associations for Rheumatology (ILAR) classification criteria [ 13 ], JIA has been classified into seven categories based on clinical features, pathogenesis, treatment, prognosis, and outcomes of the disease. The prevalence of JIA subtypes varies among different ethnic groups. Systemic arthritis was more common in Asia, especially in Thailand, China, and Japan, at approximately 37–54%, whereas oligoarthritis was most found in Western countries [ 14 – 18 ]. Patients with enthesitis-related arthritis (ERA) are more prevalent in Southeast Asia, India, and Taiwan than in Western countries [ 15 , 19 , 20 ]. This might be related to a high frequency of HLA-B27 in these regions. Rheumatoid factor (RF)-negative polyarthritis was more frequent in North America and Europe and lowest in Southeast Asia, whereas RF-positive polyarthritis was more frequent in Latin America [ 15 ]. Unlike adult-onset psoriatic arthritis (PsA), juvenile-onset PsA was uncommon in children in all regions. Uveitis, one of the complications in JIA, is commonly found in oligoarthritis with positive anti-nuclear antibody (ANA). It is more common in White children, specifically in Northern America and European countries, than in Asia [ 15 , 21 ]. However, the true prevalence of uveitis was underestimated in some countries, especially in limited resource settings, due to a lack of regular ophthalmologic screening [ 22 ]. Untreated uveitis can lead to blindness, one of the major disabilities associated with JIA. Outcomes of JIA in each region around the world vary depending on several factors. Worse outcomes in JIA are seen in Hispanic, African American populations in the USA, and also observed in Thai children in Asia. [ 18 , 23 , 24 ]. A previous study reported that patients in countries with lower GDP experienced more disease damage compared to those in higher-income countries [ 15 ]; this is likely largely explained by a lack of specialists, delay in diagnosis and access to care. Given the shortage of pediatric rheumatologists in many regions around the world, several of these studies have reported delays in accessing pediatric rheumatologists, which ultimately leads to poorer outcomes for JIA patients. Childhood-onset systemic lupus erythematosus (cSLE) Childhood-onset systemic lupus erythematosus (cSLE) is a multisystem autoimmune disease, the most common connective tissue disease in children. The prevalence and incidence rates of cSLE vary around the world. Previous studies revealed that the incidence of cSLE ranges between 0.3–0.9 per 100,000 children and 3.3–24 per 100,000 children in the prevalence [ 25 – 29 ]. Although cSLE is less common than adult-onset SLE, it is more severe and aggressive than adult-onset SLE [ 30 ]. In terms of global distribution, it has been observed that cSLE exhibits a higher prevalence and greater severity in regions such as Asia, Southeast Asia, Latin America, and Africa [ 31 – 35 ] in comparison to Western countries such as the United States (US) and Europe [ 25 , 36 – 38 ].The most common clinical manifestation of cSLE in Asia was arthritis, followed by mucocutaneous, hematologic, and renal involvement [ 39 ]. Around 60–80% of cSLE in Asia had lupus nephritis, and 38–54% were class IV [ 39 ]. African cSLE patients showed a higher propensity for developing lupus nephritis, as well as a higher probability of progressing to end-stage renal disease compared to their North American counterparts [ 40 – 42 ]. A previous study in South Africa showed that the majority of patients had mortality associated with renal disease, whereas in the US, SLE patients died from infections [ 43 , 44 ]. This may be due to constrained availability of renal replacement and transplant therapy in South Africa [ 45 ]. Furthermore, hydroxychloroquine (HCQ) use in this study was low, although HCQ has benefits in SLE patients, including higher rates of remission, fewer relapses, and diminished organ damage [ 46 ]. However, it remains uncertain whether it derives from a lack of healthcare professional expertise, the financial burden associated with medication, or adherence to the treatment [ 44 ]. India, one of the most populous LRIC showed a higher mortality rate resulting from uncontrolled disease activity, potentially caused by a delay in diagnosis and referral to specialists [ 47 ]. Previous studies reported that around 39–65% of SLE patients have damage in at least one organ system [ 48 – 52 ], and the most frequent organ damage contributing to morbidity and mortality were in neuropsychiatric, renal, ocular and MSK organ systems [ 26 , 53 – 56 ]. The ten-year survival rates were found to be lower in LRICs and MRICs at around 80–83%, in comparison to high-resource income countries (HRICs) with survival rates ranging from 95–99% [ 54 , 57 – 62 ]. Another study revealed that the 5-year and 10-year pooled survival estimates for HRICs were 0.99 and 0.97 for the 2008–2016 period, respectively, while the corresponding values for LRICs and MRICs were 0.85 and 0.79, lower than those in HRICs [ 31 ]. These studies support the evidence from quantitative and qualitative studies that delays to care in LRIC increase the risk for organ damage and mortality in cSLE patients. Disparities in Access to Pediatric Rheumatologic Care Over the past few decades, pediatric rheumatology has benefited from major therapeutic and diagnostic advances that have completely changed the prognosis of young patients suffering from MSK diseases [ 63 ]. Unfortunately, not all patients have the same access to specialized facilities with multidisciplinary teams offering quality care. This disparity of access can vary from one continent to another, or from one country to another within the same continent, or sometimes from one region to another within the same country. It is a widespread global problem, not just confined to LRICs, and is influenced by various factors such as remote geography, extreme climates, cultural beliefs, and inadequate transportation and communication networks [ 22 , 64 ]. However, the disparity in access to appropriate healthcare is particularly severe in developing countries. Seventy-eight percent of children with rheumatic disease are living in resource-poor regions in Asia and Africa [ 65 ]. In parts of these regions, children are unable to obtain basic needs and services, including clean water, sanitation, adequate nutrition and shelter, access to essential medicines, health care, and education [ 66 ]. Consequently, the LRIC healthcare budgets prioritizes nutrition, basic health services, addressing urgent conditions, especially infectious diseases such as tuberculosis, HIV, and malaria, which often takes precedence over the management of non-communicable diseases, including MSK health [ 5 ]. This prioritization exacerbates the already limited availability of specialized care for pediatric rheumatologic conditions, leaving many children without timely diagnosis and treatment [ 67 – 69 ]. As the healthcare budgets can be extremely limited, the capacity to build and maintain infrastructure for basic rheumatology care has not been prioritized [ 70 ]. Even the most talented, well-trained rheumatologist is limited by poor access to diagnostics [ 71 ]. There is a need to establish recommendations and protocols with less costly interventions to be more appropriate to daily practice in these regions. Juvenile arthritis management in less-resourced countries (JAMLess) consensus recommendations are useful in JIA management in less-resourced countries [ 68 ]. To address this essential need, the Paediatric Society of the African League Against Rheumatism (PAFLAR) ( https://paflar.org/activities ) and the Asia Pacific League of Associations for Rheumatology (APLAR) ( https://aplar.org/ ) have launched projects to derive consensus for JIA management in their respective regions. Cultural influences also shape access patterns to pediatric rheumatologic care in LRICs and MRICs. The stigma surrounding chronic illnesses, including rheumatic conditions, may contribute to delayed diagnosis and treatment-seeking behaviors [ 71 , 72 ]. Traditional beliefs and practices may also influence perceptions of disease, leading to underutilization of medical services. Furthermore, cultural norms around gender may impact access to care, with girls potentially facing additional barriers due to societal expectations or discrimination. The absence of identified and well-established early referral strategies in the healthcare system exacerbates access challenges to pediatric rheumatologic care [ 72 ]. This often leads to delayed referrals from primary care providers, which in turn can cause missed opportunities for early intervention. Consequently, advanced disease progression and poorer outcomes may occur. Previous research has identified five categories influencing the outcomes of JIA: biology, individual behaviors, social environment, physical environment, and health services [ 73 ]. Disparities have been observed among racial and ethnic minorities of JIA patients and those from low socioeconomic backgrounds. Therefore, when developing care plans and healthcare policies, it is crucial to consider sociocultural factors to improve outcomes for this population [ 73 ]. The lack of a dedicated curriculum in pediatric rheumatology has prompted some initiatives to address clinical care gaps. In Kenya, focus groups identified some interventions to empower non-specialist healthcare workers to provide effective care [ 74 ]. Patient-focused measures involve education and psychosocial support, while community efforts include awareness campaigns and financial assistance mobilization. Healthcare worker interventions encompass diagnostic and management guidelines, along with research and educational initiatives. Strengthening healthcare systems is emphasized to improve insurance coverage and access to comprehensive multidisciplinary care. These interventions collectively aim to bridge gaps in pediatric rheumatology care, ensuring timely and effective support for patients across Kenya [ 74 ]. The workforce shortage is another limiting factor for ideal care. The disparity in healthcare workers is massive; regions with the highest burden of disease, particularly Africa and Southeast Asia, are often those with the fewest healthcare workers per capita [ 66 ]. The paucity of pediatric rheumatologists in these regions reflects these severe regional shortages of healthcare personnel and pediatricians in general [ 75 ]. This imbalance was exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic [ 75 ]. The recommended ratio of pediatric rheumatologists in each population is 1:300,000 to 1:500,000, and the US has 1 per 250,000 children [ 22 , 67 ]. In contrast, only ten pediatric rheumatology centers exist in 54 African countries [ 65 ]. Sub-Saharan Africa has 16 trained pediatric rheumatologists, of whom ten are in South Africa (1 for every 37 million children) [ 22 ]. Asia has one pediatric rheumatologist for every 26 million children; India has one for every 30 million children [ 22 , 67 ]. While telemedicine is considered to alleviate this issue [ 64 ], it is not perceived as a viable solution for addressing the scarcity of resources and geographical barriers to accessing pediatric rheumatology centers of care. First, an initial visit with a pediatric rheumatology provider to make a diagnosis and establish a relationship is ideal before initiating telemedicine services. Unlike in HRICs, there is limited acceptance among parents of telemedicine as a care model [ 76 ]. This highlights the critical need for education initiatives targeting parents and patients to promote telemedicine, particularly in the field of pediatric rheumatology [ 76 ]. As a result of patients’ lack of awareness, other traditional healers or health professionals such as general practitioners, family physicians, or orthopedists get involved instead of pediatric rheumatologists [ 77 ]. Hence, it is important to raise awareness among general and specialized practitioners about MSK examination skills and define uniform case ascertainment or referral criteria [ 77 ]. Exemplar models of care such as Rightpath, Miracle Feet, and Tin Soldiers have developed novel methodologies to facilitate the identification and diagnosis of MSK conditions ( https://www.pmskglobal.com/models-of-care ). Furthermore, e-technologies in education and training are also useful tools for achieving this goal, like the successful experiences in teleteaching educational sessions organized by PAFLAR, APLAR, and Paediatric Rheumatology European Society (PReS) [ 22 , 65 , 78 ]. Pediatric Musculoskeletal Matters (PMM), a free e-resource (with content from global partners, global initiatives for MSK curricula, and rheumatology training programs), is a highly effective tool in countries where specialist care is lacking [ 79 ]. Addressing disparities in access to pediatric rheumatologic care requires a multifaceted approach that addresses systemic challenges within healthcare infrastructure, economic inequality, cultural factors, and issues of early referral. Investing in healthcare resources, raising awareness among healthcare providers and communities, and promoting early detection and intervention are essential to ensure that all children, regardless of their background or location, have equitable access to high-quality rheumatologic care [ 79 ]. Challenges in Diagnosing Pediatric Rheumatologic Diseases Prompt referral to a pediatric rheumatology center enables effective and appropriate management, undoubtedly modifying the natural history of rheumatic diseases thus improving their prognosis [ 80 ]. Many epidemiological data demonstrated that patients living in LRICs and MRICs had greater disease activity and damage than those living in wealthier countries, and damage has been associated with referral delay [ 15 , 81 ]. Studies have shown that the diagnosis of JIA is most frequently made by pediatric rheumatologists, who then initiate adequate therapy during the window of opportunity [ 82 ]. International guidelines advocate that whatever the country’s income level, new patients with suspected JIA should be evaluated by a pediatric rheumatologist within four weeks from the time of referral children or/and ten weeks from symptoms onset [ 83 , 84 ]. Literature reports indicate a median time to access pediatric rheumatologists of 3 to 10 months [ 85 ]. Limited access to pediatric rheumatologists has been identified as a global challenge [ 86 ]. In fact, in many developed countries, despite the development of pediatric rheumatology units, diagnosis has been delayed [ 87 ]. Reasons for delayed referral are multifactorial [ 88 ] include healthcare system settings and facilities, intensity and nature of symptoms, whether the patient lives in the city center or rural area, distance of the patient’s residence to the pediatric rheumatology center, social representation of the disease, or the educational and cultural background of parents, and the level of a financial burden on patients [ 77 , 85 , 89 , 90 ]. Lack of awareness of rheumatic MSK disorders among healthcare providers is a major contributor in setting with limited exposure to rheumatology training and care. Routes of referral to pediatric rheumatology care are often complex, involving primary care, such as emergency medicine, general pediatrics, and family medicine, and secondary care, such as general pediatrics, adult rheumatologist, and orthopedics. Most first line health workers are unfamiliar with pediatric rheumatic diseases because pediatric MSK medicine is not core teaching in medical schools [ 64 , 91 – 92 ]. The major challenge is identifying and understanding the barriers and drivers in the referral process. One thing is certain: raising awareness about childhood-onset MSK diseases is the key, particularly among the public and healthcare professionals. Healthcare planners need to work in partnership with clinicians, training bodies, and academic institutions to create an integrative care model between the children, general practice, and the pediatric rheumatology team with interdisciplinary care coordination across the healthcare system [ 22 , 64 ]. Healthcare providers, policymakers, non-profit organizations, and patient organizations need to collaborate to create and maintain resources with open and equitable access [ 22 ]. Inter-regional and international collaboration can promote shared learning and improve the global MSK care quality [ 93 ]. Another barrier to optimal care in LRICs and MRICs is the poor access to advanced diagnostic tools such as musculoskeletal ultrasound (MSUS), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), immunologic tests, and medicines related to their availability and affordability [ 15 ]. These results can lead to a significant burden on the early diagnosis and effective monitoring of rheumatic diseases in pediatric patients. Moreover, the ideal therapeutic goal is to obtain disease remission, which can be achieved by using approved and effective antirheumatic medications [ 94 ]. However, access to certain biological, disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) can be limited in LRICs due to cost or supply chain issues. It may be attributed to the context of the economic landscape [ 15 , 95 ]. The scarcity of these diagnostic modalities and high-cost medications stems from various factors, including resource constraints, infrastructure limitations, and a shortage of trained healthcare professionals specializing in rheumatology. Disparities in Treatment Options One of the primary obstacles to achieving inactive disease without permanent organ damage such as limb length discrepancy, joint damage, blindness, delayed growth and development, osteoporosis, and complications such as macrophage activation syndrome, which contribute to the high mortality rate in systemic JIA and SLE, is access to effective therapies, including biologic DMARDs. There are however, significant disparities in treatment options between LRICs and MRlCs [ 96 ]. Despite being located in Southeast Asia, Singapore stands out from neighboring countries as it has access to all biologic therapy for appropriate disease indications [ 97 ]. In contrast, in Laos, Myanmar, Nepal, and Vietnam, where there is a scarcity of well-trained pediatric rheumatologists, access to biologic agents is limited, to only a few or is not available [ 98 ]. Further, the affordability of these medications is another issue. Biologic therapeutics are not in the National Essential Drug lists in most nations, leading to a lack of government support and lack of access, even when the drugs may be available in their countries. Macroeconomic conditions explain the differences in access to biologic agents between Eastern and Western European countries with Eastern European countries tending to use biologic agents less than others due to unaffordability [ 99 ]. This trend is also observed in Africa, the Middle East, and Southeast Asia [ 15 ]. Examining regional variations further underscores the complexities of access to pediatric rheumatologic care in Africa [ 67 , 69 ]. Economic factors play a significant role, with disparities between urban and rural areas exacerbating healthcare inequalities. In urban centers, healthcare facilities may be relatively better equipped, but accessibility remains a challenge for many families due to financial constraints or inadequate transportation infrastructure. Conversely, rural communities often face even greater barriers, including limited access to healthcare facilities and shortages of qualified medical personnel. Even in HRICs like the US, the accessibility of biologic agents is affected by the area where patients reside. Patients in urban and metropolitan areas tend to have better access to biologic agents compared to those in rural areas [ 100 ]. Aside from economic problems, the shortage of healthcare professionals, especially pediatric rheumatologists, might be one reason why only a few biologics are available. Laos is among the Southeast Asian countries lacking pediatric rheumatologists and facing a shortage of conventional DMARDs like azathioprine, cyclophosphamide, leflunomide, and sulfasalazine. In contrast, most countries in the region have access to these medications [ 98 ]. Additionally, in LRICs and HRICs alike, lack of economic support or insurance coverage for healthcare services, including medications, is often limited or nonexistent for large population segments. These factors contribute to disparities in access to essential medications and healthcare services, particularly for vulnerable populations. This underscores the urgent need for improved healthcare financing mechanisms and policies to ensure equitable access to medications [ 98 , 101 ]. Establishing a Global Task Force for Pediatric Musculoskeletal Health (GTF) and special working groups within the ACR, PReS, PAFLAR, and APLAR focused on global health issues have generated further impetus for collaborative efforts to improve MSK outcomes. An illustrative example of the impact on policy can be found in the GTF’s successful efforts to bring together a representative international group to advocate for improvements in the World Health Organization (WHO) Essential Medicines List for Children (EMLc). There is a need to increase the medications available for pediatric rheumatic diseases, particularly in LRICs, at a policy level. The WHO Essential Medicines List (EML) and EMLc are useful policy documents that inform the National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM) committee to improve healthcare planning for priority diseases. By ensuring resources such as the WHO EML and EMLc are up to date [ 102 ], standard care and improved access and availability of medicines may be provided by the NLEM committee. In 2022, the GTF surveyed the medicines that should be included in the WHO EML for treating JIA [ 103 ]. The survey involved 173 responders from 46 countries worldwide, with approximately 70% being pediatric rheumatologists with around ten years of clinical practice experience. The responding physicians deemed that methotrexate should be included in the WHO EML, while around 90% advocated for the inclusion of intra-articular steroids. Tumor necrosis factor (TNF) inhibitors, particularly adalimumab, were identified as the most needed biologic agents, followed by interleukin (IL)-6 and IL-1 inhibitors [ 103 ]. Despite intra-articular steroid injections being a fundamental procedure, only half of the respondents reported that they are always available; primary barriers cited were lack of timely access to day-case facilities, which encompassed general anesthesia, sedation, and the lack of imaging availability for performing intra-articular steroid injections, such as ultrasound guidance. Interestingly, concerns were also raised regarding geographic issues, where patients residing in remote areas had to travel to hospitals solely for joint injections. Affordability remains a significant barrier to accessing these medications. The majority of survey respondents reported that the affordability of medicines used in standard pediatric rheumatology practice, in particular, the biological agents, was a significant barrier to care and especially where treatment costs were not covered by health care insurance policy [ 103 ]. The challenge of accessing biologic agents in LRICs and MRICs has led to the development of pragmatic guidelines based on the availability and affordability of these medications in each region. For instance, initiatives like the JAMLess recommendations had been developed [ 68 ]. In Thailand, IL-1 inhibitors are not available and regulations do not permit the use IL-6 inhibitors as first-line medication per the ACR 2021 guidelines for SJIA treatment [ 104 ] due to economic issues. Consequently, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and steroids are required to be used for at least six months before considering tocilizumab. Patients are therefore exposed to predictable side effects such as delayed growth, obesity, infections, and osteoporotic fractures from prolonged use of steroids. Those with severe morbidity also tend to have fewer opportunities to obtain high-quality jobs compared to patients without such health issues. Currently, APLAR is in the process of developing clinical practice guidelines specifically tailored for LRICs and MRICs in Asian regions. As far back as 2000, the WHO director declared: “Although the diseases that kill attract much of the public’s attention, MSK or rheumatic diseases are the major causes of morbidity throughout the world, having a substantial influence on health and quality of life, and enormous burden of cost on health systems” [ 105 ]. Even more than 20 years later, there remains a significant lack of awareness and knowledge about pediatric rheumatic diseases among healthcare professionals. Consequently, there is a lack of funding, policy and support in various areas, including pediatric rheumatology fellowship training and access to medications [ 106 ]. While the cost-effectiveness and budget impact of novel medications are major topics of discussion in HRICs, the unaffordability of these expensive medications in LRICs receives limited attention from governments [ 107 ]. In LRICs, the higher drug costs with relatively low returns on investment often fail to meet cost-effectiveness criteria, thereby preventing access to these medications [ 107 ]. This could lead to poor health status among the population of these countries, creating a vicious cycle that affects their economic system. Therefore, improving the availability of biologic agents necessitates the development of the entire healthcare system to allow for equitable access to therapy. Role of Educational Initiatives in Bridging the Gap Enhancing healthcare for children with rheumatic diseases requires collaboration among healthcare professionals at all levels. A shortage of pediatric rheumatology education for medical students, pediatric residents, and general physicians also impacts patient care in the pediatric rheumatology field and delays the diagnosis and treatment of these patients [ 66 , 92 , 108 ]. There is a significant education capacity gap, with fewer pediatric postgraduate training programs available in regions with low pediatrician density. The limited opportunities are statistically influenced by region and GDP [ 109 ]. A previous study conducted in Southeast Asia and Asia-Pacific revealed that approximately 60% of these countries lack a pediatric rheumatology training program. The main barriers identified were a shortage of trained pediatric rheumatologists to supervise the trainees, followed by a lack of interested applicants and insufficient funding for the trainees [ 98 ]. The acquisition of knowledge among healthcare personnel has been identified as a contributing factor to delays in diagnosis and the implementation of improper therapeutic strategies. Conversely, the field of pediatric rheumatology is characterized by its complexity and a lack of educational resources, particularly in LRICs. These circumstances often lead to feelings of helplessness, frustration, and a lack of confidence among healthcare personnel when treating children with rheumatic diseases [ 110 ]. Therefore, in countries with a shortage of pediatric rheumatologists, general pediatricians or adult rheumatologists typically care for children with rheumatic diseases. Africa had the fewest pediatric rheumatology training programs available (with training programs limited to South Africa for all of Sub-Saharan Africa), followed by Southeast Asian countries. One explanation is the scarcity of pediatric rheumatologists in these regions, with physicians who act as pediatric specialists in their countries not having completed fellowship training programs. Instead, they have acquired informal training and experience in specific fields [ 75 ]. Pediatric rheumatology is not yet recognized as a distinct subspecialty by most African and Southeast Asian countries. This lack of recognition stems from a general unawareness of the potential severity of MSK diseases in children and the absence of dedicated training in this field. This situation is unsurprising, considering that pediatric rheumatology has been acknowledged as a subspecialty in the US since the 1960s but only officially recognized in 1990. Even in the US, there exists a gap between the demand for patient care and the availability of services provided by trained pediatric rheumatologists [ 111 , 112 ]. Approximately 90% of pediatric rheumatologists are situated in large cities, necessitating that children with rheumatic diseases in remote areas travel long distances to access pediatric rheumatologists [ 113 , 114 ]. Although the number of pediatric rheumatology training positions in the US has increased, one-fourth of fellowship positions remain unfilled due to a lack of trainee awareness of these opportunities or insufficient funding [ 113 ]. Despite the numerous challenges, successful developments in the curriculum have been achieved in some regions. For instance, a postgraduate degree entitled “Musculoskeletal diseases of children and adolescents” was created for the first time at the Tunis El Manar University Faculty of Medicine of Tunis this academic year 2021–2022. This degree program addresses the training needs of various disciplines involved in managing MSK diseases in children. It could offer a new perspective in Tunisia extending to French-speaking Africa ( https://www.fmt.rnu.tn/ ). Another notable example comes from Southeast Asia, where the first pediatric rheumatology training program in Thailand was established more than ten years ago by the Royal College of Pediatricians of Thailand. This pediatric rheumatology fellowship training program was developed by four well-trained Thai pediatric rheumatologists who graduated from North America. Currently, Thailand can produce pediatric rheumatologists who can serve in various parts of the country, with the majority of them receiving their training in Thailand. Nevertheless, a 2021 APLAR Pediatric Rheumatology Special Interest Group survey with 18 from 33 member nation organizations revealed that only 20 pediatric rheumatology training programs in 13 countries are available [ 78 ]. The main barriers to developing conventional structured training programs are the lack of opportunities for formal training, including travel expenses and high training costs. Teleteaching educational sessions could be an alternative method to enhance education for physicians, including general practitioners, general pediatricians, and pediatric and adult rheumatologists, especially in countries where fellowship training programs are unavailable. Therefore, in 2021, PAFLAR has launched a series of monthly webinars focusing on key topics within the field of pediatric rheumatology. These webinars serve as platforms for disseminating knowledge, sharing insights, and fostering collaboration among professionals in the field. It covers a range of important subjects and provides an invaluable opportunity for healthcare practitioners, researchers, and stakeholders to stay updated on the latest advancements, best practices, and emerging trends in pediatric rheumatology. Through these webinars, PAFLAR plays a crucial role in advancing the understanding and management of rheumatic diseases in children across the region ( https://www.paflar.org/activities ). In the same year, APLAR also introduced case-based webinars, each featuring presentations from two countries within APLAR members (79). These webinars are highly beneficial to the audiences, as presenters review and summarize the topic based on case scenarios. Subsequently, there are discussions, questions, and suggestions from experts within and outside APLAR member nation organizations. APLAR also launched a single-topic course on autoinflammatory diseases, where attendees must answer questions correctly at least 80% of the time to receive certificates endorsed by APLAR. Additionally, a single-topic course on vasculitis is currently ongoing. Educational activities appear to be further developing on the European front. PReS aims to promote pediatric rheumatology as a subspecialty across European countries and expand its reach to international membership. Its objectives include enhancing patient care through knowledge and networks to raise awareness, facilitating high-quality research to generate new knowledge, and supporting education and training initiatives [ 115 ]. PReS’ educational activities encompass various programs tailored to different audiences. PReS Basic Courses target general pediatricians and pediatric residents, especially in LRICs and MRICs and have to date been held in India, Brazil, Hungary, South Africa, and Malaysia. Advanced courses cater to more experienced audiences, including pediatric rheumatology fellows and pediatricians interested in the field [ 22 , 115 ]. PReS Specialist Skills Courses combine knowledge-based Advanced Courses with hands-on workshops, such as Musculoskeletal Ultrasound (MSUS) and joint examination skills using the pediatric Gait Arms Legs and Spine screening examination (pGALS) method [ 116 ]. The Sister Hospital Initiative is another PReS project that aims to support hospitals and healthcare professionals in LRICs by pairing them with pediatric rheumatology teams in other countries. This initiative fosters a two-way exchange of knowledge, educational activities, experiences, and patient care across different settings, considering the diverse healthcare systems found in each region ( https://www.pres.eu/education/sister-hospital.html ). Additionally, PReS offers online resources, notably the Paediatric Musculoskeletal Matters (PMM) website ( https://www.pmmonline.org ), which covers basic clinical skills and approaches to investigations and initial management of MSK problems. These resources are targeted at medical students and family medicine doctors and include a section for nurses [ 117 ]. PReS also recently launched the first PReS knowledge-based test, aiming to provide a standardized assessment of knowledge in pediatric rheumatology. This examination is open to candidates worldwide and serves as an international benchmarking tool. This could be beneficial for practitioners from regions that lack exit examinations in pediatric rheumatology ( https://www.pres.eu/pres2023/KBE.html ). Furthermore, PReS supports young pediatric rheumatologists and researchers through programs like the Young Investigators Meeting (YIM) and the PReS EMERGE (Emerging Rheumatologists and rEsearchers) fellowship program ( https://www.pres.eu/activities/young-investigators/fellowship-programs.html ). This initiative provides financial support to clinical trainees under the age of 40 who are members of PReS, to gain clinical knowledge, skills, and research experience for up to six months at European Paediatric Rheumatology Centers. Nowadays, there are better opportunities to enhance pediatric rheumatology knowledge in LRICs and MRICs through e-technologies, including online conferences, webinars, websites, and short-course training. These resources make it more accessible for attendees, although financial support to attend such events remains a major barrier [ 22 ]. However, the most crucial step in enhancing patient care in countries lacking pediatric rheumatologists is developing a curriculum for pediatric rheumatology fellowship training in those countries or neighboring countries. Research Gaps in Pediatric Rheumatology At present, there remains a lack of literature on pediatric rheumatic diseases, especially the epidemiology of common pediatric rheumatic diseases such as JIA and its subtypes. There were only reports from single centers or particular regions. Little is known about pediatric rheumatic diseases and the disparity between different geographical areas or ethnic groups [ 14 ]. As the majority of pediatric patients reside in LMICs, and treatment protocols vary due to limited access to care and medications compared with HRICs, it will be beneficial to understand the prevalence, treatment approach, and outcomes of the diseases in these regions as well. Due to a shortage of healthcare professionals, the working systems between LMICs and HRICs differ significantly. Lack of data from many parts of the world is a pervasive problem, and one which prevents estimation of the global burden of pediatric rheumatic disease. The majority of the world’s pediatric population lives in LMIC, regions with few or no pediatric rheumatology providers [ 71 ]. The disparities in global access to rheumatology care, education, and training are reflected in global access to rheumatology research. Indeed, it is not possible to conduct pediatric rheumatology research while lacking a clinician able to diagnose the patients. In LMICs, physicians often fulfill multiple roles, including patient care, education, and research, in contrast to their counterparts in HRICs, who can focus on areas of interest. Consequently, a significant proportion of pediatric rheumatology publications originate from HRICs more than LRICs [ 118 ]. Beyond time constraints and heavy workloads that impede research efforts in LMICs, insufficient research funding is a major obstacle. Research efforts require trained personnel, infrastructure, tools and databases relevant to the study. Retrospective studies can be done with less funding, requiring a scientist with training to review charts, enter and analyze data. This is overly burdensome for sites with few or no providers. Prospective studies, clinical trials, and translational research require even more resources, and funding is a common barrier to these types of research in LMICs [ 119 ]. Given our shortage of research teams, collaborative efforts that fund research assistants for data collection and technicians for laboratory work would significantly enhance our research output. The continued growth and development of several important research groups and organizations, such as PRINTO, PReS, and the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance (CARRA), have worked to overcome the difficulties of increasing standardizing research practices and generating larger cohorts for the study of therapies, outcome measures and epidemiological aspects of rheumatic MSK diseases [ 120 ]. However, there is still a lack of clinical trials involving LRICs, where the clinical setting in these areas differs from HRICs in several aspects, including delay in access to healthcare and medications leading to poorer outcomes or difficult-to-treat or higher infection rates such as tuberculosis [ 60 ]. Clinical trials are designed to demonstrate the efficacy and safety of medications and the outcomes of diseases. Most of the data came from studies of Western European and North American nations, which may not be applicable to the lower income settings with different populations and infection risks [ 15 ]. The Pediatric Rheumatology International Trials Organization (PRINTO) ( https://www.printo.it ) is an exemplar of collaboration between HRICs and LMICs. The multinational study of the EPidemiology, treatment and Outcome of Childhood Arthritis (EPOCA study), which investigates the global epidemiology of JIA across 49 countries spanning five continents, exemplifies this collaboration. One research component that is part of the EPOCA study, titled “Phenotypic Variability and Disparities in Treatment and Outcomes of Childhood Arthritis throughout the World: An Observational Cohort Study [ 15 ],” offers extensive data on JIA subtype prevalence and medication usage across various regions worldwide. This study underscores disparities, revealing that countries with lower GDP exhibit greater disease activity and damage compared to their high-income counterparts, with damage correlating with referral duration. While these studies focus on global disparities, it remains challenging for principal investigators to emerge from LMICs. In addition to comprehending epidemiological studies on pediatric rheumatic diseases, there are numerous other aspects we should be able to understand better. These include genetic susceptibility, disease mechanisms, environmental influences, disease burden, quality of life, treatment options, and disease outcomes across various regions globally [ 118 , 121 ]. Translational research has historically focused on populations from HRICs. The lack of inclusion of diverse patients in translational research decreases the chance of meaningful discovery in a large proportion of the world’s pediatric population. This narrow scope of translational research limits the depth and breadth of our mechanistic understanding of disease. We believe that with support from HRICs in research studies, including research funding and collaboration networks, which encompass data collection assistants and assistance in experiments where there is a shortage of laboratory equipment, as well as the expansion of study settings in LRICs, particularly in clinical trials, we can achieve a better understanding of pediatric rheumatic diseases across various clinical settings. This can eventually lead to the development of universal and standardized data collection to advance international collaboration. These efforts can also develop treatment protocols which account for efficacy and risk for specific populations around the globe. Real-world experience in pediatric rheumatology development Given the disparities in various aspects, such as patient care, including access to diagnostic and treatment services, as well as education and research gaps, we have compiled the issues and proposed solutions for pediatric rheumatology development in Table 1 . The sections below provide examples of real-world experiences in the development of pediatric rheumatology. The involvement of all stakeholders in pediatric rheumatology development is summarized in Figure 1 . Table 1. Challenges and strategies in pediatric rheumatology development Challenges Strategies Patient Care Limited/delay to access to subspecialist Educate MSK diseases among healthcare providers Raise awareness of MSK problems among communities and policymakers Online consultation or telemedicine Lack of pediatric rheumatologists Produce pediatric rheumatologists by developing fellowship training programs or training abroad Upskill other MSK healthcare workers (Pediatricians, Orthopedic Surgeons, or Adult Rheumatologists) to be more familiar with juvenile arthritis Limited access to advanced diagnostic tools Collaborate with other hospitals/institutions Limited access to high-cost medications Raise awareness to policymakers Address unfair patent laws that prevent the development of biosimilars or generic agents. Perform cost-effectiveness project Add high-cost medications to the National Lists of Essential Medicines in your country Establish the treatment guidelines in LRICs/MRICs Lack of awareness (healthcare providers, patients, policymakers, public) Raise awareness of MSK problems among healthcare providers, patients, communities, and policymakers Education Lack of pediatric rheumatology education for all levels: medical students, pediatric residents, general physicians, general pediatricians, orthopedic surgeons Integrate pediatric rheumatology knowledge into the curriculum at all levels Provide teleteaching educational sessions Provide short course training or conferences for healthcare providers Lack of pediatric rheumatology fellowship training Develop pediatric rheumatology fellowship training in your own countries or collaborate with other institutions/international centers Encourage/support pediatric rheumatologists as a leader in developing curriculum Barriers to attending the international conference (financial, time-consuming, workloads, language barrier) Free online international conference or educational website e.g. PMM online, pGALS Funding support or fee reduction for pediatric rheumatologists in LRICs/MRICs Invite pediatric rheumatologists in HRICs to be a visiting professor in LRICs/MRICs Lack of support from the national educational organization, including funding for fellowship position Raise awareness among national educational organizations and policymakers, such as Ministry of Public Health Lack of awareness or interest among trainees for a fellowship position Encourage medical students and pediatric residents to train in pediatric rheumatology Research Multiple roles and heavy workloads that impede research efforts Seek for research assistants Produce more pediatric rheumatologists Lack of research funding Provide funding for LRICs/MRICs to do the research Lack of research assistants & laboratory equipment Seek funding e.g. international organization funding Collaborate with other institutions or international centers Lack of epidemiological data and clinical trials in LRICs/MRICs Facilitate more international collaboration, e.g., PRINTO Provide an application or website registry to collect epidemiological data Support pediatric rheumatologists in LRICs/MRICs to conduct clinical trials research Open in a new tab Figure 1. Open in a new tab Key stakeholders in pediatric rheumatology development. Conclusion In the development of pediatric rheumatology globally, the focus needs to be on three factors: patient care, education, and research, as these aspects often interconnect. Improving patient care necessitates the development of an education system to train more pediatric rheumatologists, particularly in regions with limited or no specialists in the field. Furthermore, understanding the natural course of diseases, appropriate therapy for local populations, and risks in LRICs is vital for enhancing patient care, highlighting the importance of research. Support from international organizations, funders, and specialized working groups, such as the GTF, significantly enhances pediatric rheumatology in these resource-limited settings. However, local efforts are equally essential in developing pediatric rheumatology within individual regions. Raising awareness of MSK diseases among healthcare providers, policymakers, and communities is crucial, as it can lead to improvements in healthcare infrastructure, such as increased accessibility to high-cost medications, funding support, and fellowship opportunities. Improved awareness to seek health care and patient advocacy is important to optimize outcomes for children and young people. Collaborative efforts among HRICs, MRICs, and LRICs are also imperative to reduce disparities in pediatric rheumatology on a global scale. Figure 2. Open in a new tab The steps of Thailand’s educational development in pediatric rheumatology. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Thailand’s model: the steps to access to high-cost medications. *PR, pediatric rheumatology, Rx, treatment, NLEM, National List of Essential Medicines A case study from Thailand: from small steps to a first success. Educational development Approximately 15 years ago, Thailand had only one pediatric rheumatologist (SV) who had completed fellowship training in the US. She faced an overwhelming demand for patient care, providing consultations every day of the year and receiving referrals from across the country. Her initiative began with integrating pediatric rheumatology into the medical student curriculum and residency training program, ensuring that future general physicians and pediatricians would be knowledgeable about pediatric rheumatic conditions. Subsequently, a pediatric rheumatology fellowship training program was established with the support of the Faculty of Medicine at Ramathibodi Hospital, Mahidol University. Within two years, this program yielded two additional pediatric rheumatologists who joined as faculty members, facilitating the expansion of the training program along with three well-trained pediatric rheumatologists from other institutions who had recently graduated from Canada. The new program has been supported by the Royal College of Pediatricians of Thailand. It operates across two training centers: the Faculty of Medicine at Ramathibodi Hospital and the Faculty of Medicine at Siriraj Hospital, both within the same university. Moreover, we delivered knowledge on pediatric rheumatic diseases both within and beyond our institution, covering various regions of Thailand. At the same time, we identified and encouraged pediatric residents with an interest in pediatric rheumatology to pursue fellowship training. Therefore, new pediatric rheumatologists are trained each year, many of whom assume roles as lecturers in medical schools, educational centers, or advanced-level hospitals across Thailand. We summarize the steps of academic development in Figure 2 . Currently, the Pediatric Rheumatology Society of Thailand consists of around 24 members, most of whom received training in Thailand. Those who have served as lecturers pursued further training in North America or European countries afterward. Last year, we successfully established our pediatric rheumatology workshop for general pediatricians. The workshop drew significant interest from many pediatricians, who expressed that it had benefited them. They have expressed their desire for us to continue providing such workshops or seminars in the future. However, this number of pediatric rheumatologists remains insufficient to meet patient demand. The key barriers include inadequate government funding and a lack of awareness among public healthcare leaders regarding the importance of pediatric rheumatology. Despite interest from pediatricians, limited funding often directs them toward other subspecialties. Patient care development. However, another significant barrier lies in accessing high-cost medications like biologic agents for treating JIA. Thailand’s healthcare system does not cover these expensive medications, making it impossible to adhere to the ACR guidelines for JIA treatment [ 104 , 122 ]. In Thailand, children have two healthcare coverage options: the Civil Servant Medical Benefit Scheme (CSMBS) for those with parents working as government officers and the Universal Coverage Scheme (UCS) for all other children. Initially, we included TNF inhibitors and IL-6 inhibitors in the CSMBS, allowing JIA patients access to these medications under this scheme. However, most patients fall under the UCS, which only covers medications only in Thailand NLEM, excluding these biologic agents due to their newness and high cost. Fortunately, the Health Intervention and Technology Assessment Program (HITAP), a non-profit organization under Thailand’s Ministry of Public Health ( https://www.hitap.net/en ), conducts research to evaluate the cost-effectiveness of high-cost medications like biologic agents. We led the first project on the cost-effectiveness of tocilizumab in SJIA treatment. Although the findings suggested tocilizumab might not be cost-effective overall [ 123 ], another research in Thailand indicated it could significantly improve outcomes for SJIA patients, particularly when administered early [ 124 ], and this medication can save patients’ lives. Therefore, after approximately seven years of processes, tocilizumab was approved for inclusion in Thailand NLEM for SJIA treatment after conventional therapies have failed. This ensures access for SJIA patients under the UCS, similar to those under the CSMBS. Another medication, TNF inhibitors, was assessed for cost-effectiveness in non-SJIA patients. With the recent launch of cheaper TNF inhibitors, biosimilars of adalimumab, in Thailand, our research demonstrated its cost-effectiveness for non-SJIA patients. As a result, in 2024, healthcare policymakers approved adalimumab into Thailand NELM for non-SJIA patients, enhancing accessibility for patients with UCS. These experiences in Thailand may offer insights into healthcare systems in other countries facing similar challenges. We summarize the steps of Thailand’s model in accessing high-cost medication in Figure 3 . Practical points. Pediatric rheumatology should advance in many ways, including through education, patient care, and research as these are inter-connected. A thorough understanding of the real causes or obstacles in your country or region is necessary when planning and developing pediatric rheumatology services. Although the contexts may change, some challenges are shared between different income and resourced regions of the world. Global networks support the development of pediatric rheumatology regionally and worldwide. Research agenda. Create systems to collect data needed in LRICs/MRICs to understand the real situations better. Prioritize strategies to address obstacles to do research in these regions including the workload of physicians, lack of research teams, facilities, and funding. HRICs are called to support researchers from LRICs and MRICs in many ways, including collaboration and funding support, particularly epidemiologic data, randomized controlled clinical trial. Acknowledgments We would like to thank Professor Cláudia Saad Magalhães (Pediatric Rheumatology Unit, Department of Pathology, Botucatu Medical School, Sao Paulo State University, Botucatu, São Paulo, Brazil) and the members of the Pediatric Global Musculoskeletal Task Force (GTF) for inspiring and supporting us to write this review article. We appreciate Dr.Nicola Aldridge (Translational and Clinical Research Institute, Newcastle University, UK) for their work on the WHO survey and on the GTF. Furthermore, we would like to acknowledge Assoc. Prof. Sirirat Charuvanij (Rheumatology Division, Pediatric Department, Faculty of Medicine Siriraj Hospital, Mahidol University, Bangkok, Thailand), a program director of another training center, as one of a key person in developing pediatric rheumatology fellowship training programs in Thailand. Declaration of competing interest Dr. Lewandowski is funded by the NIAMS Intramural Research Program. References [1]. Kumar B. Global h