umatol. 2022;81:487–491. doi: 10.1007/s00393-022-01191-8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Pfeil A, Baerwald CGO, Sieburg M, Boche K, Kupka TA, Linde T, Heldmann F, Unger L, Oelzner P, Aringer M, Keysser G. Future of rheumatologists: what are the perspectives? Survey of resident physicians in rheumatology in middle Germany. Z Rheumatol. 2020;79:168–174. doi: 10.1007/s00393-019-00713-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Pfeil A, Fleck M, Keyßer G. Specialist training situation in rheumatology from a trainer’s perspective. Z Rheumatol. 2021;80:22–26. doi: 10.1007/s00393-020-00922-z. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Pfeil A, Krusche M, Vossen D, Berliner MN, Keyßer G, Krause A, Lorenz HM, Manger B, Schuch F, Specker C, Wollenhaupt J, Baraliakos X, Fleck M, Proft F. Model curriculum of the German Society for Rheumatology for advanced training in the discipline internal medicine and rheumatology. Z Rheumatol. 2021;80:688–691. doi: 10.1007/s00393-021-01053-9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Riemekasten G, Aringer M, Baerwald CG, Meyer-Bahlburg A, Bergner R, Feuchtenberger M, Gebhardt C, Hellmich B, Keysser G, Lorenz HM, Kneitz C, Witte T, Muller-Ladner U, Schneider M, Braun J, Rautenstrauch J, Specker C, Schulze-Koops H. Rheumatology—Integration into student training (RISA): current structure of clinical rheumatology in German universities (RISA III) Z Rheumatol. 2016;75:493–501. doi: 10.1007/s00393-016-0079-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Smith E, Hoy DG, Cross M, Vos T, Naghavi M, Buchbinder R, Woolf AD, March L. The global burden of other musculoskeletal disorders: estimates from the Global Burden of Disease 2010 study. Ann Rheum Dis. 2014;73:1462–1469. doi: 10.1136/annrheumdis-2013-204680. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Zink A, Braun J, Gromnica-Ihle E, Krause D, Lakomek HJ, Mau W, Muller-Ladner U, Rautenstrauch J, Specker C, Schneider M. Memorandum of the German Society for Rheumatology on the quality of treatment in rheumatology—Update 2016. Z Rheumatol. 2017;76:195–207. doi: 10.1007/s00393-017-0297-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',103,'2025-10-15 08:43:46','2025-10-15 08:43:46');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3613,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087090','Pilot study of reproductive health counseling in a pediatric rheumatology clinic.','Ronis T, Frankovich J, Yen S, Sandborg C, Chira P.','2014','headss headss relinquish experimenting condom teen teen teen lucile packard assented headss headss mnemonic ducation ctivities rugs exual uicide headss pmd mctd menarchal noncommittal packets teen papanicolau teen teen 2t32ar050942 06a1 ctsa ul1 rr025744 ncrr ronis ronis frankovich sandborg chira ronis ronis frankovich chira radzik sherer neinstein prepregnancy prams gavin summ elk elster passo beukelman kone paut piram mecacci pieralli paidas semperi semperi santucci akers borrero teratol bdra bdra britto passo rohrbaugh archfami hedberg andresen s1054 139x goldenring saraiya','Abstract Objective To assess perception and behavior after reproductive health counseling among adolescent patients in a tertiary care-based pediatric rheumatology clinic. Methods Adolescent females seen at Stanford pediatric rheumatology clinic were prospectively enrolled during routine visits. At study start, standard clinic procedures for the following were reviewed with providers: 1) HEADSS (home, education, activities, drugs, sexual activity, and suicide/depression) assessment; 2) reproductive health counseling; and 3) medical record documentation. Patients were enrolled if providers indicated that they performed HEADSS assessment and reproductive health counseling. At enrollment, patients completed a survey to assess perceptions of reproductive health counseling. Chart review confirmed documented discussions. Follow-up survey 3-5 months after enrollment tracked reproductive health information seeking behavior. Results Ninety females (ages 17 ± 2 years old) participated. Almost all patients (99%) agreed that reproductive health was discussed. Seventy-one percent reported that pregnancy risks were discussed, 42% had recent concerns about reproductive health, and 33% reported their provider recommended that they seek further reproductive health care. Eighty-four patients completed follow-up phone surveys, with 25% reporting seeking further information on reproductive health concerns but merely 9.5% actually sought further care. Only 18% reported having ever asked their rheumatology provider for guidance regarding reproductive health care concerns. Conclusion Routine reproductive health discussion and counseling are necessary in a rheumatology clinic; as in our experience, a substantial number of adolescents have concerns and actively seek reproductive health information. Despite these discussions, teens rarely pursued further reproductive health care. Further work to bridge this gap is needed. Introduction Adolescence is a unique period of rapid biological, social, emotional, and intellectual growth 1 . The psychosocial developmental process can be especially challenging for adolescents with chronic rheumatic diseases as they are encouraged to achieve self-care and independence, potentially causing conflict between teens and providers. For instance, adolescents may refuse medications with cosmetic effects despite understanding the benefits of treatment, or parents and providers may be unwilling to relinquish control of care to the adolescent for fear of adverse consequences. During this time many teens exhibit the impulsive decision-making of childhood, while experimenting with risky behaviors and not adhering to therapy 2 . Typical adolescent behaviors can include unsafe sexual practices. The 2011 National Youth Risk Behavior Surveillance of high school students conducted by the Centers for Disease Control showed that 46% of high school females had ever had sex, 46% of sexually active females had not used a condom during their last sexual intercourse, and 78% of sexually active females did not use hormonal contraception before their last sexual intercourse 3 . These behaviors can have negative outcomes such as sexually transmitted infections and unplanned pregnancies, and the United States has the highest teen pregnancy rate of any developed country (estimated 400,000 teens aged 15-19 giving birth annually) 4 . Teen mothers are more likely to receive late or no prenatal care, have a preterm or low birthweight infant, and experience negative social outcomes, including school dropout 4 , 5 . Pregnancy rate data among adolescent rheumatology patients are lacking but unprotected sexual activity is a recognized clinical problem. Yearly screening of adolescent sexual health is recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) and American Medical Association (AMA) and is a quality measure for optimal care of patients with Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (JIA) 6 – 8 . Pediatric rheumatology patients often take teratogenic or cytotoxic medications (e.g., methotrexate, leflunomide, mycophenolate mofetil, and cyclophosphamide,) contraindicated during pregnancy. Some rheumatic diseases (such as lupus) are reportedly more severe in adolescence with higher frequency of aggressive renal disease, requiring steroids and cytotoxic drugs 9 . Should unplanned pregnancy arise, the teen is typically advised to stop these medications to decrease risks to the developing fetus. Unfortunately, discontinuation of medicines and/or pregnancy itself can cause disease flare. Patients with active disease are more likely to have complications with pregnancy such as spontaneous abortion, intrauterine growth restriction, or preterm delivery 10 , compounding the risks of adolescent pregnancy. Women aged 18-45 felt counseling regarding potential teratogenic risks of medications should occur when the medication is prescribed 11 . Since pediatric rheumatologists often see adolescents more frequently than their primary care physicians and are the prescribers of these medications, the rheumatology clinic visit represents an important opportunity to effectively screen, counsel, and promote good reproductive health to prevent adverse pregnancy outcomes. Research on reproductive health in pediatric rheumatology patients has been limited with one study demonstrating physician screening practices could be improved with an educational intervention 12 . No study has reported the correlation between adolescents’ perception about reproductive health screening and recommendations and physician documentation of this assessment, but literature has shown that physicians and adult patients often give discrepant reports about the events during a clinic visit 13 . Factors leading adolescents to seek further information and care regarding reproductive health have not been evaluated. The quality, effectiveness, and outcomes of reproductive health counseling have been challenging to assess 14 . The goal of this study is to assess adolescent rheumatology patients’ perception and behavior after reproductive health counseling in the rheumatology clinic. Our overall objective is to foster positive reproductive health outcomes among adolescents with rheumatic diseases by improving reproductive health counseling in the pediatric rheumatology clinic. Materials and Methods Study population A convenience sample of consecutive patients were invited to participate in the study if they met the following inclusion criteria: female sex, age 13-20 years, English speaking, diagnosis of a rheumatologic condition, >2 rheumatology clinic visits at Lucile Packard Children''s Hospital at Stanford, and provider indication that the patient had received the routine reproductive health screening and counseling during the visit on enrollment day. IRB approval was obtained and all patients and at least one parent/guardian assented/consented to participate. Reproductive health screening and counseling Prior to study start, all clinic providers were instructed in the use of the HEADSS assessment, counseling tool, and medical record documentation and were encouraged to follow these standard clinic practice guidelines and documentation practices for all pediatric rheumatology patients. HEADSS is a psychosocial risk assessment instrument that has been in clinical use since 1974 and is a mnemonic for h ome, e ducation, a ctivities, d rugs, s exual activity, and s uicide/depression. It addresses the major areas of adolescent psychosocial stress and is a practical clinical screening instrument 15 . We developed a standardized clinic counseling tool that addressed topics of confidentiality, pregnancy risk in the context of the rheumatic disease, teratogenic risks of medications, contraception, and referral to reproductive health services ( Table 1 .) Eligible patients were screened and counseled with a paper HEADSS assessment and counseling tool that was included in the medical record by the rheumatology provider as part of their routine clinic visit. Table 1. Reproductive Health Counseling Tool ✓ Discussed need for contraception ✓ Recommended double barrier contraception if sexually active ✓ Discussed adolescents'' right to confidential reproductive health care ✓ Risks of pregnancy in context of rheumatic disease ✓ Risks of potentially teratogenic medications: ✓ Need for planning prior to pregnancy in conjunction with medical provider ✓ What to do if worried about being pregnant ✓ Worries about future fertility ✓ Referred: □ Adolescent Medicine □ gynecologist □ PMD □ Planned Parenthood Open in a new tab Data Collection and Presentation Patients completed a questionnaire in clinic, directly after enrollment, on the day of the reproductive health screening and counseling. The initial questionnaire assessed patient-provider trust, patient''s perceptions of whether counseling had occurred, their general knowledge about medications and disease and whether the patient had recent reproductive health concerns. Follow-up phone surveys occurred 3-5 months after the initial enrollment and assessed whether the patient had sought further reproductive health information or care and their perception of talking about reproductive health with the rheumatologist. Charts were reviewed for demographic and medical data as well as for documentation of reproductive health screening. All questionnaires were pilot-tested to ensure that language was appropriate and understandable. Data are presented descriptively. Results Ninety patients were enrolled and completed an initial survey (from December 2010 -March 2011.) All 90 participants had a rheumatologic disease, and 15 (17%) had a concurrent pain syndrome. Patient diagnoses, medication use, and demographic data are included in Table 2 . Ninety-four percent had documentation of reproductive health counseling in the medical record. Table 2. Baseline patient characteristics Patient Characteristics n % Total 90 Age (SD) (yrs) 17 ± 2 Diagnosis JIA 41 45.6 SLE/MCTD/Sjogren''s 36 40 Vasculitis 4 4.4 Juvenile Dermatomyositis 3 3.3 Scleroderma 2 2.2 Other 4 4.4 + Pain syndrome 15 16.7 Disease duration (SD) (yrs) 5 ± 4 Race/Ethnicity Non-Hispanic white 45 50 Hispanic 22 24.4 Asian/Pacific Islander 18 20 Non-Hispanic black 3 3.3 Unanswered 2 2.2 Insurance status Private 70 77.8 Public 20 22.2 Medication use Remission off medications 5 5.6 Current high risk medication use 61 67.8 History of high risk medication use 74 82.2 Current high-risk medications biologics 21 23.3 mycophenolate 17 18.9 methotrexate 16 17.8 azathioprine 11 12.2 leflunomide 2 2.2 cyclophosphamide 1 1.1 tacrolimus 1 1.1 Development Post-menarchal 88 97.8 Open in a new tab Of the 90 patients enrolled, 23 (26%) had engaged in sexual intercourse and 3 (3%) were considering sexual activity. Eleven (12%) were already followed by another provider for reproductive health care. Almost all patients (99%) agreed reproductive health was discussed and 88 (98%) felt confidentiality was assured. Sixty-four patients (71%) reported that pregnancy risks of medications were discussed. All patients agreed they felt comfortable enough with the provider to ask questions. Eighty-eight patients (98%) understood the need to take medications for disease control. Almost half (38 patients, 42%) had recent concerns about reproductive health (which included pregnancy, sexually transmitted infections, or contraception issues.) One third of the patients (30 patients, 33%) reported their provider recommended that they seek further reproductive health care. Of those patients who were recommended by their provider to seek further reproductive care, 10 (33%) were already followed by another provider for reproductive health care, 2 (6.7%) said they would definitely go, 12 (40%) planned to go in the future, 5 (17%) were noncommittal and 1 (3%) did not answer. See Figure 1 . Figure 1. Patient reproductive health seeking. Open in a new tab Of the 30 patients who initially reported that they were advised by their rheumatologist to seek further reproductive health care in the initial survey; only 3 patients did so at follow-up. Two of those patients had said that they would plan to go in the future and one had said that they would definitely seek further care. Follow-up phone calls were completed in 84 patients (93%) with a median interval of 136 days after the initial survey (range 115-185 days.) Of the 30 patients who initially reported that they were advised by their rheumatologist to seek further reproductive health care in the initial survey, only 3 patients did so at the follow-up call. Of the 84 who completed the follow-up call, 30 (36%) had concerns and questions about reproductive health over the past 3 months. Twenty-one patients (25%) reported seeking further information about sex or reproductive health (since their initial visit) from family, internet, friends, school, other doctors, and the rheumatologist. Thirty-one patients (37%) reported seeking further information about their condition or medications (since their visit) from the internet, rheumatologist, other doctors, family, and information packets. Fifteen patients (18%) reported having ever asked their rheumatology clinic provider for guidance regarding relationships or sex. Eight patients (10%) reported seeking additional reproductive or sexual health services since their visit at Planned Parenthood clinics, family doctors, gynecologists, and school clinics. Only one patient stated that she sought further care based on a rheumatologist''s recommendation. Of the 8 patients who sought further reproductive health care, 7 were currently on high risk medications (OR 3.63, 95% CI 0.4 to 31.0.) See Figure 2 for the distribution of patients who expressed health concerns at follow-up, and who actually sought care. Figure 2. Overlap of patients with reproductive health concerns who sought further care. Open in a new tab At the initial visit, 30 patients were recommended to seek further reproductive health care. At follow-up, 3 of these patients actually sought further care while 20 still reported having reproductive health concerns. Discussion This is the first study investigating patient perception of reproductive health counseling and its potential to effect behavior change in a pediatric rheumatology clinic. Our study indicates that a considerable proportion of teen rheumatology patients (42%) have recent reproductive health concerns but few necessarily follow through with a formal visit with a reproductive health care professional when counseled to do so. While teens do acknowledge counseling about reproductive health occurs during these physician-patient interactions, they may not have the know-how to obtain the necessary reproductive health services and interventions. This highlights the potential need for a different counseling tool, a formal referral process, or an additional intervention such as having a combined clinic with adolescent gynecology. Different settings may also be helpful to introduce the subject which may include having parents (mothers) be a part of the counseling process or a confidential peer session to review the information. Patients taking high risk medications may require extra guidance compared to those not on high risk medications. For example, a new federal mandate in the United States requires that patients prescribed mycophenolate-containing compounds receive reproductive health counseling; however there are no specific guidelines for adolescent rheumatology patients. Changes in guidelines now recommend that screening by Papanicolau (Pap) test should not be used for women aged <21 years, regardless of initiation of sexual activity 16 . This may lower the barrier for pediatric rheumatologists who are comfortable in doing so to prescribe oral contraception and may reduce the need for more than annual gynecological exams from another provider. Making reproductive health discussion a routine process may help open communication and provide better understanding of patient motivation to seek further care. Training in core competencies of contraception counseling as a requirement in pediatric rheumatology fellowship is one way of implementing provider education, as well as potentially including it as a quality improvement initiative for maintenance of certification for rheumatology boards. Study limitations This study was limited by small sample size, its exclusion of males, and only enrolling English speaking families. Male patients do need counseling about reproductive health and may require a different intervention than females. Non-English speaking patients of Hispanic and Asian descent who comprise a significant proportion of patients at Stanford may have different cultural beliefs than the studied population. Patients attending the clinic at Stanford reflect the demographics of Santa Clara County (which includes the city of San Jose), which has a higher median income and higher percentage of Asian and Hispanic populations than other major US cities. Our data may not be generalizable to other metropolitan centers with different demographics (e.g. more African-Americans or lower median incomes) that may have higher likelihood of sexual activity at a younger age but may also have different reproductive health seeking behaviors and resources. For the purposes of this study, data about sexual history, contraception, and information about fertility and gonadal protection were not collected but may be important variables in future research. Respondents potentially knew the interviewer conducting phone calls which could have influenced results as a way to please the investigator. This observational study demonstrated the importance of reproductive health counseling in pediatric rheumatology. Future studies could include the development of a more standardized and effective counseling tool and referral process. There may also be a role for partnerships with other community service providers, such as adolescent clinics, to facilitate education and services, as well as trying to create a format to include other people in the conversation such as peers and/or parents to reinforce positive behaviors and follow through. Significance and Innovations. This is the first study investigating patient perception of reproductive health counseling and subsequent follow up in a pediatric rheumatology clinic. Teen rheumatology patients are a high risk group for adverse reproductive health outcomes. Many teen rheumatology patients have recent reproductive health concerns. Few adolescent rheumatology patients follow through with a formal visit with a reproductive health care professional. Acknowledgments The work for this study was supported by NIH T32 grant 2T32AR050942-06A1 and data were stored in a secure Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) database supported by a Stanford Center for Clinical Informatics grant (Stanford CTSA award number UL1 RR025744 from NIH/NCRR.) There is no financial support or other benefits from commercial sources for the work reported herein. The authors deny any other financial interests which could create a potential conflict of interest or appearance of such with regard to the work. Footnotes Author Contributions All authors were involved in drafting the article or revising it critically for important intellectual content, and all authors approved the final version to be submitted for publication. Dr. Ronis had full access to all of the data in the study and takes responsibility for the integrity of the data and the accuracy of the data analysis. Study conception and design. Ronis, Frankovich, Yen, Sandborg, Chira. Acquisition of data. Ronis. Analysis and interpretation of data. Ronis, Frankovich, Chira. References 1. Radzik M, Sherer S, Neinstein LS. Adolescent Health Care: A Practical Guide. Fifth Edition Lippincott Williams & Wilkins; Philadelphia, PA: 2007. Psychosocial development in normal adolescents. pp. 27–31. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Dean AJ, Walters J, Hall A. A systematic review of interventions to enhance medication adherence in children and adolescents with chronic illness. Arch Dis Child. 2010;95(9):717–723. doi: 10.1136/adc.2009.175125. doi:10.1136/adc.2009.175125. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Centers of Disease Control and Prevention Youth Risk Behavior Surveillance — United States, 2011. MMWR. 2012;61(4):24–27. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Centers of Disease Control and Prevention Prepregnancy Contraceptive Use Among Teens with Unintended Pregnancies Resulting in Live Births — Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System (PRAMS), 2004–2008. MMWR. 2012;61(2):25–29. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Gavin L, MacKay AP, Brown K, et al. Sexual and reproductive health of persons aged 10-24 years - United States, 2002-2007. MMWR Surveill Summ. 2009;58(6):1–58. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Hagan J. Bright futures: guidelines for health supervision of infants, children, and adolescents. 3rd ed. American Academy of Pediatrics; Elk Grove Village IL.: 2008. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Elster A. American Medical Association. AMA guidelines for adolescent preventive services (GAPS): recommendations and rationale. Williams & Wilkins; Baltimore: 1994. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Lovell DJ, Passo MH, Beukelman T, et al. Measuring Process of Arthritis Care. A Proposed Set of Quality Measures for the Process of Care in Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis. Arthritis Care Res (Hoboken) 2011;63(1):10–16. doi: 10.1002/acr.20348. doi:10.1002/acr.20348. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Kone-Paut I, Piram M, Guillaume S, Tran TA. Lupus in adolescence. Lupus. 2007;16(8):606–612. doi: 10.1177/0961203307079562. doi:10.1177/0961203307079562. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Mecacci F, Pieralli A, Bianchi B, Paidas MJ. The impact of autoimmune disorders and adverse pregnancy outcome. Semin. Perinatol. 2007;31(4):223–226. doi: 10.1053/j.semperi.2007.05.005. doi:10.1053/j.semperi.2007.05.005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Santucci AK, Gold MA, Akers AY, Borrero S, Schwarz EB. Women''s perspectives on counseling about risks for medication-induced birth defects. Birth Defects Res. Part A Clin. Mol. Teratol. 2010;88(1):64–69. doi: 10.1002/bdra.20618. doi:10.1002/bdra.20618. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Britto MT, Rosenthal SL, Taylor J, Passo MH. Improving rheumatologists’ screening for alcohol use and sexual activity. Arch Pediatr Adolesc Med. 2000;154(5):478–483. doi: 10.1001/archpedi.154.5.478. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Rohrbaugh M, Rogers JC. What did the doctor do? When physicians and patients disagree. Arch Fam Med. 1994;3(2):125–128. doi: 10.1001/archfami.3.2.125. discussion 129. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Hedberg VA, Klein JD, Andresen E. Health counseling in adolescent preventive visits: effectiveness, current practices, and quality measurement. J Adolesc Health. 1998;23(6):344–353. doi: 10.1016/s1054-139x(98)00043-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Goldenring JM, Rosen DS. Getting into adolescent heads: an essential update. Contemporary Pediatrics. 2004;21(1):64–92. [ Google Scholar ] 16. Saraiya M, King J, Thompson T, et al. Cervical Cancer Screening Among Women Aged 18-30 Years -- United States, 2000-2010. MMWR: Morbidity & Mortality Weekly Report. 2013;61(51/52):1038–1042. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',79,'2025-10-15 08:43:59','2025-10-15 08:43:59');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3614,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9529788','Rheumatology Clinic Staff Needs: Barriers and Strategies to Addressing High Blood Pressure and Smoking Risk.','Tong M, Gilmore-Bykovskyi A, Block L, Ramly E, White DW, Messina ML, Bartels CM.','2022','overlooks improperly saldana nowell familiarizing qsr brainstormed agb schedulers cautioned forwarding recheck p15 p15 p17 stickers stickers theehr p10 printout p17 pamphlets phenomenal patien loud p15 verbiage recheck p15 downplay sketchy uninterest excuses excuses rushing automaticity rechecking p15 p19 stagnated notifies p12 stickers stickers circling recheck p15 stickers printouts pamphlets folders packet packet cautioned packet verbiage metered recheck bug p15 uninterest indifferent excuses insisting p17 uninterest forwarding chantix inhalers wellbutrin emulate primdahl enablement triangulate ctsa 1ul1tr002373 gundersen rincon avina zubieta sadatsafavi yurkovich vostretsova choy ganeshalingam semb chobanian bakris vasan leip ambrose barua keeffe farewell myles r270 keeling agca heslinga rollefstad semb rollefstad riel vidrine shete bodenheimer perilous mcglynn primdahl huizinga choy soubrier perrodeau comedra saldana nowell wattiaux bettendorf carayon schoofs hundt karsh seips i50 ramly bondar primdahl clausen horslev cutolo knaus manojlovich decicco mcgrail glessner ramly stroik lauver previsit ramly strouse tob prochaska diclemente norcross sykes aimer treharne stebbings stralen','Abstract Objective: Patients with rheumatologic conditions are at elevated risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) due to inflammatory and traditional risk factors, such as high blood pressure (BP) and smoking. However, rheumatology clinics rarely address traditional risk factors, even though they are routinely assessed and modifiable in primary care. The present study sought to 1) characterize rheumatology clinic staff’s work process for addressing high BP and smoking and 2) identify barriers and strategies for effective management of these risk factors. Methods: We conducted seven focus groups with medical assistants, nurses, and scheduling staff from four adult rheumatology clinics across two health systems (BP focus groups, n=23; smoking, n=20). Transcripts were analyzed using thematic analysis to elucidate barriers and strategies. Results: We found three clinic work processes for the management of high BP and smoking risk: 1) risk identification, 2) follow-up within the clinic, and 3) follow-up with primary care and community resources. Within these processes, we identified barriers and strategies grouped into themes: 1) time, 2) clinic workflows, 3) technology and resources, 4) staff’s attitudes and knowledge, and 5) staff’s perceptions of patients. The most pervasive barriers were 1) no structured system for follow-up and 2) staff confidence and skill in initiating conversations about health-related behavior change. Conclusion: Our study identified generalizable gaps in rheumatology staff’s work processes and competencies for addressing high BP and smoking in patients. Future efforts to support staff needs should target 1) systems for follow-up within and outside the clinic and 2) conversation support tools. Keywords: Health Services, Cardiovascular Diseases, Blood Pressure, Tobacco, Risk Factor INTRODUCTION Patients with rheumatologic conditions are at higher risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) than peers [ 1 - 4 ]. Elevated blood pressure (BP) and smoking exacerbate CVD risk [ 5 - 7 ] and rheumatologic damage [ 8 ], yet many patients in rheumatology do not routinely receive adequate CVD care management to address these risk factors [ 9 , 10 ]. The European League Against Rheumatism recommends periodic CVD risk assessments [ 11 ], but approaches to implement this recommendation are not standardized, data driven, or widely used [ 12 , 13 ]. Experts recommend the use of primary care referrals for CVD risk management to improve BP control and address smoking cessation [ 12 , 13 ]. Protocol-driven care has also been shown to improve smoking cessation referrals [ 14 ]. However, existing barriers to care coordination between specialty and primary care settings often preclude patients from receiving preventive treatment [ 15 , 16 ]. Many patients with rheumatoid arthritis identified their rheumatologist as their “main” provider, yet rheumatologists reported competing priorities and unclear roles for CVD risk management [ 17 ]. Despite the critical need for rheumatology clinics to partner in CVD management, previous research largely overlooks prevention practices in rheumatology or non-vascular specialty clinics, instead placing the burden on primary care. Recent literature shows that some rheumatology nurses are already engaging in basic CVD management, such that additional interventions would be relevant and appropriate [ 18 ]. Another study on nurse-led individual comorbidity management visits in RA demonstrated clear benefits to patients [ 19 , 20 ]. Short of such intensive, individualized CVD assessments that require extended use of nurses’ time, rheumatology may also call on non-nursing clinic staff to support CVD risk management. We build on the existing literature to examine how rheumatology clinic staff can address gaps in CVD risk management. The objective of our study was to characterize current clinic staff work processes for addressing high BP and smoking to identify barriers and strategies for effective risk management. Findings will inform developing effective, tailored CVD risk management protocols for rheumatology specialty clinic staff. MATERIALS AND METHODS Recruitment. We recruited nurses, medical assistants, and scheduling staff from four adult rheumatology clinics in two health systems to participate in focus groups about existing specialty clinic practices for BP and smoking cessation risk management. Figure 1 summarizes our recruitment and data collection methods. We chose staff as our primary data source since they engage with patients more frequently than providers and are already responsible for clinic workflows involving checking BP and smoking status. The Institutional Review Board (IRB) certified exemption, waiver of individual written consent, and permission to publish this improvement initiative. Participants provided verbal informed consent for focus group audio recordings. Figure 1. Flow diagram for focus group recruitment and data collection on clinic staff needs for addressing blood pressure (BP) and smoking cessation. Open in a new tab Abbreviation: MAs = medical assistant Data Collection. Two trained facilitators led one-hour focus groups with clinic staff participants to explore current workflows related to high BP and smoking. Seven focus groups with 25 distinct participants were conducted in total, with four groups discussing blood pressure and three groups discussing smoking cessation ( Fig. 1 ). We used a semi-structured interview guide to ask participants a series of questions about 1) the process of identifying high BP or smoking, 2) related follow-up, and 3) general experiences working with patients to address these risk factors. To better understand barriers and strategies, we also probed about the timing and consistency of clinic workflows, perceived staff roles, ease of communication, and cases of absent communication or action. Focus group interviews were audio recorded. All rheumatology staff voluntarily participated in at least one focus group. At one health system, we paid participants a stipend of $40 for after-hours focus group participation, while the other health system conducted focus groups during paid clinic time. Analysis. Focus group recordings were transcribed verbatim; one focus group was improperly recorded, so facilitator notes were used for analysis. We conducted thematic analysis using well-established methods outlined by Braun, Saldana, and Mays [ 21 - 23 ]. To ensure trustworthiness in qualitative research, we also followed the six phases outlined by Nowell et al: 1) familiarizing with data, 2) generating initial codes, 3) searching for themes, 4) reviewing themes, 5) defining and naming themes, 6) producing the report [ 24 ]. Analysis was conducted with NVivo 11 software (QSR International). A preliminary coding scheme leveraged prior work involving rheumatologist and primary care provider communication in CVD prevention for RA patients [ 17 , 25 ]. The present scheme drew on the work systems model from human factors engineering, which recognizes that health outcomes are shaped by processes within complex work systems [ 26 ]. Notably, our study methodologically paralleled process mapping for intervention development in other work by our study group [ 27 ]. First, a member of the research team (MT) who was not involved in the data collection reviewed a subset of transcripts and the preliminary coding scheme. This coder then brainstormed additional codes with guidance from two experienced researchers (CB, AGB) and structurally coded the subset of transcripts. Two research team members (MT, CB) then reviewed and discussed all coded passages, revising codes as discrepancies arose. Once codes were finalized, the first coder (MT) documented all code definitions in a codebook and finished coding all transcripts. A second experienced researcher, not involved in the initial code development (LB), used the codebook to independently code all transcripts. This further ensured coding reliability and consistency. Discrepancies were adjudicated by consensus. Inter-rater agreement between the two coders (MT, LB) was measured with greater than 90% agreement across all relevant codes. Finally, from this process, analytic themes were iteratively distilled from aggregated code labels. As themes emerged, they were finalized through recurring discussions with the study team (MT, CB). RESULTS Clinic Site Overview Study sample. A total of 25 unique staff participated in focus groups, with 23 participants discussing BP management and 20 discussing smoking. Table 1 characterizes participants by role and site. Table 1. Focus Group Characteristics * Blood pressure Smoking MA 8 10 RN 8 8 Float/Schedulers 7 2 Health System 1 15 13 Health System 2 8 7 Total 23 20 Open in a new tab * Several participants had experience working in float roles or had current or prior multispecialty clinic exposure. Medical assistants performed most pre-visit rooming. Differences between the two health system practices for BP and smoking risk management included availability of smoking cessation materials and EHR prompts. One of four clinics described cessation resources and recently adopting readiness-to-quit assessments as a clinic standard. Three reported rarely assessing readiness-to-quit, despite having EHR functionality for many years. Clinic Work Processes for Blood Pressure and Smoking Risk Management Across both systems, staff identified three shared clinic work processes that addressed high BP and smoking risk management: A) identification of high BP or smoking, B) follow-up within the clinic, and C) follow-up across settings, such as primary care and community resources. Consistency and intensity of engaging in these processes varied by clinic and individual staff. Staff check-in routines included rooming patients, measuring vitals, and initiating conversations about prior high BP or smoking. Standard timing for checking BP varied. Routines for assessing smoking varied less, yet some staff cautioned that this was, “a little more robotic… a check in the box… rather than actually expressing that you care….” Follow-up within the clinic involved communication between clinic staff and rheumatologists. Although not uniformly used, one clinic described staff-to-physician communication informing the entire team of a high BP. Similar communication standards were not described for smoking follow-up. Follow-up outside the clinic to address high BP or smoking was described at some clinics via referrals to primary care and community resources. In one instance, staff described forwarding telephone notes about high BP to primary care, although this was not consistent. Barriers and Strategies to Blood Pressure and Smoking Risk Management We identified ten barriers and seven strategies related to clinic staff’s execution of BP and smoking risk management. Tables 2 - 3 organize the barriers and strategies into five themes: 1) time, 2) clinic workflow, 3) technology and resources, 4) rheumatologists’ and staff’s attitudes and knowledge, 5) staff’s perceptions of patients’ attitudes and knowledge. Of the ten barriers, three involved identification of high BP or smoking, five involved follow-up within the specialty clinic, and two involved follow-up with primary care and community resources (i.e., state Quit Line). Of the seven strategies, two involved the clinic work process of identifying high BP or smoking, four involved follow-up within the specialty clinic, and one involved follow-up with primary care and community resources. Figure 2 outlines a flow diagram that maps barriers and strategies onto the three core clinic work processes. Table 2. Structural barriers and strategies to blood pressure (BP) and smoking risk management in specialty clinics Theme Barriers Strategies 1. Time 1. Time constraints during rooming A BP: “I mean we barely have time to get patients in… and then sometimes the providers come in when we’re still rooming.” [P1] SMOKING: “I felt good that day about asking [about readiness to quit], it really was just random.” [P2] BP: “I’s already almost automatic, I think, for me to be like, ‘Your blood pressure is a little high’ or ‘Your blood pressure is normal’ …right after I take it.” [P13] SMOKING: Not described. 2. Clinic Workflow 2.1 Structure for follow-up procedures B BP: “There is no system thing that everybody does… we’re like, ‘Hey, did we ever recheck that BP? I noticed it was really high.’ ‘Well, that was like 2 hours ago, and I didn’t know that you wanted me to.’” [P15] SMOKING: “I just feel like we can be better about flow … into the follow-up after the ‘Are you ready to quit?’ Where do you go from there?” [P7] BP: “So in those cases where they do have a high BP and you work as a team … [The nurse will] talk to the doctor. They will kind of decide on, ‘Okay, well go back and check it.’” [P15] SMOKING: “With checking a patient in, you ask if they are a… current smoker or former smoker.” [P1] 2.2 Communication among staff, rheumatology, and primary care C BP: I know just from past experience I’ve… heard [physicians] say, ‘Make sure you follow up with your primary,’ but they leave it with the patient.” [P17] SMOKING: “I usually just say, ‘Do you want any information on quitting?’… And there’s been people who have said yes, but if it’s in a specialty department where their PCP isn’t there, it never gets addressed. ” [P6] BP: “We have a great tool of [EHR name]… I’ve actually sent a telephone note with elevated BP readings to the PCP because then it’s just easier. They can look at it when they have a chance versus calling, just faster.” [P5] SMOKING: “There are some clinics that actually will schedule with a nurse tobacco cessation appointment for like an hour so they can sit down and talk and go through it.” [P6] 3. Technology and Resources 3.1 Clinic tools A BP: “We give our providers stickers, so if it’s a high BP, we could write it on the stickers. I don’t know that we always do though. ” [P13] SMOKING: “ I don’t know what to put in [theEHR] …they’re ready to quit or no?” [P6] BP: Not described. SMOKING: “There’s a part built right into our [EHR] that they have to address… You have to address the tobacco.” [P10] 3.2 Educational materials C BP: “They’re going to get’a printout of what their blood pressure, their vitals were in their after-visit summary.” [P17] SMOKING : “We do have a tobacco cessation folder, and it’s got pamphlets. It’s got different resource material…but there’s really no follow-up.” [P9, 10] Open in a new tab Theme’s connection to work process from Figure 2 : A Identification of high BP or smoking B Follow-up within the specialty clinic C Follow-up across settings (i.e., primary care or community resources) Table 3. Personnel-related barriers and strategies to blood pressure (BP) and smoking risk management in specialty clinics Theme Barriers Strategies 4. Rheumatologists’ and staff’s attitudes and knowledge 4.1 Rheumatologists’ perceptions of roles and responsibilities B BP: “A lot of times they [rheumatologists] are like, ‘That’s not my area, primary can take care of that. ’” [P14] SMOKING: “Maybe [patients]… don’t realize it can worsen their actual disease and make their symptoms worse… maybe if they had more of that… maybe they do get that from the doctors… but sometimes it doesn’t seem like it.” [P9] BP: “Some rheumatologists play primary too. Like [Dr. X]. She’s phenomenal about managing everything - her patien’s hypertension.” [P5, 6] SMOKING: Not described. 4.2 Staff’s confidence in communication strategies B BP: “You take their BP and they’re looking at you, so you just kind of mention it out loud…” [P15] SMOKING: “ I don’t have the verbiage to continue…Even when I tried to say, ‘Are you interested? I have a card’ …I don’t know where to go with it.” [P2] BP: “I may go into great detail if it’s really high or if it’s a little off or they react to it and be like, ‘I would have the doctor recheck that before you go.’” [P15] SMOKING: “I encourage [cutting back] because obviously they’re trying to make an effort. I think that whole word, ‘Quit,’ is overwhelming to someone who has maybe done it for so long.’” [P4] 4.3 Staff’s knowledge about care standards and treatment guidelines B BP: “Well they just start asking questions. I’m not the doctor, so I don’t feel like I… just try to downplay it. ” [P7] SMOKING: Not described. BP: Not described. SMOKING: “Since I’m a smoker I know wha’s out there. But I feel like it’s kind of sketchy as far as getting into it.” [P6] 5. Staff’s perceptions of patients’ attitudes and knowledge 5.1 Staff’s perception of patients’ uninterest or discomfort A BP: “I find a lot of patients make excuses, a lot of excuses, ‘Oh, it’s always high. Oh, when I check it at home, it’s normal. Oh, you didn’t do it right.’” [P7] SMOKING: “I’ve had, ‘Don’ ask me that ever again’ …about if they’re a smoker. [P3] 5.2 Staff’s perception of patients’ knowledge about care standards B BP: “[Patients] ask… ‘What should [my BP] be?’ I hear that all the time.” [P7] SMOKING: “I think sometimes if they knew, ‘Hey… you doing this is not only bad for your lungs or your heart or whatever, but it’s worsening your [rheumatic] disease.’” [P9] Open in a new tab Theme’s connection to work process from Figure 2 : A Identification of high BP or smoking B Follow-up within the specialty clinic C Follow-up across settings (i.e., primary care or community resources) Figure 2. Open in a new tab Interactions among workflow barriers and strategies for blood pressure (BP) and smoking risk management. Numeric annotations correspond with themes in results section and Tables 2 - 3 . Theme 1: Time The first theme identified was time constraints during rooming. For BP management, one nurse explained that “[Patients] don’t come on time for their appointments and we’re rushing to get them in.” [P1] For smoking cessation, staff reported that physician needs were prioritized so that nurses needed to “Cut [check-in routines] short if the doc needs to see the patient… If we’re running behind, we just do the bare minimum.” [P2] As a result, clinic staff did not consistently follow-up with readiness-to-quit assessments after learning about a patient’s smoking history. One nurse disclosed that the readiness-to-quit conversation occurred “20% or 10% maybe, really not often,” [P2] since it was not a clinic requirement in three of four clinics. On the other hand, time constraints sometimes facilitated automaticity and routine. Although standards varied by organization; staff in some clinics described the risk management check-in process as “automatic” [P13] or as a procedure that happened “every time” as a “standard rule,” [P6] whereas staff in other clinics reported more variability. In one clinic, technology facilitated a routine, as the EHR contained a hard stop that required staff to record smoking history. Theme 2: Clinic Workflow The second theme identified was clinic workflow, which encompassed the two major domains of follow-up procedures within the clinic and communication across settings among clinic staff, rheumatology, and primary care. 2.1. Structure for follow-up procedures. Staff reported inconsistency in rechecking high BP during the visit, often citing time constraints and poor staff-physician communication. One nurse stated, “There is no system [for BP follow-up].” [P15] Another nurse recognized “We can be better about flow… into the [smoking] follow-up.” [P7] Although no clinics had formal follow-up processes for high BP or smoking risk management, some staff described a successful, consistent partnership between nurses and rheumatologists to report high BP values. One nurse stated, “If it’s high, I’ll write it and circle it, and if it’s really high I’m like, ‘Uh, Doc, you want to look.’” [P19] Another nurse described a similar partnership for following up with smoking cessation conversations, though this process has since stagnated: “When I first started here, we would get called in once in a while if someone expressed an interest in trying to quit.” [P9] 2.2. Communication among clinic staff, rheumatology, and primary care. Staff reported that rheumatologists were responsible for communicating with primary care providers, but staff frequently described uncertainty in this process. One nurse disclosed that they record high BP on paper but stated “I don’t know what [the rheumatologist] does when it’s high. I don’t know if he notifies the PCP or not.” [P12] Staff shared that some rheumatologists left follow-up to the patients with the phrase: “Let your primary care provider know.” [P2, 3] When patients did not have a primary care provider, staff reported, “The ball gets dropped.” [P6, 7] For smoking care, staff reported that they “should refer [patients] to their primary care physicians and… actually assist them with getting that first appointment made there.” [P11] However, no systematic process was described. Despite the significant structural barriers, one clinic leveraged the EHR to their advantage. A nurse from this clinic described “We have a great tool of [EHR name]… I’ve actually sent a telephone note with elevated BP readings to the PCP because then it’s just easier. They can look at it when they have a chance versus calling, just faster.” [P5] In the case of smoking management, one nurse points to role modeling by some specialty clinics: “There are some clinics that actually will schedule with a nurse smoking cessation appointment for like an hour so they can sit down and talk.” [P6] Theme 3: Technology and Resources The third theme identified was technology and resources, which encompassed both clinic tools for staff, such as stickers and EHR cues, as well as educational resources for patients. 3.1. Clinic tools. When implemented, physical reminders helped ensure high BP follow-up within the clinic. Both health systems reported using stickers, circling values on demographic sheets, and placing “flags outside each door… a universal standard for recheck.” [P15] Although staff said these practices were “system-wide,” [P14] they were also inconsistent: “I don’t know that we always [use stickers].” [P13] For smoking cessation care, staff in both health systems reported that the EHR reinforced follow-up by requiring responses for the smoking status screening question. Nurses consistently stated, “You can’t close [the EHR encounter] …it’s a hard stop for all patients,” [P4] though readiness-to-quit assessments were not a hard stop. Staff also pointed to the EHR’s inability to capture the nuances of readiness-to-quit assessments. When patients gave ambiguous answers or revealed some intention of cutting back, one nurse stated, “I don’t know what to put in [the EHR] …they’re ready to quit or no?” [P6] In another example, staff could not record cutting back on a cigarette-by-cigarette scale, as the EHR only measured usage in pack units. No EHR tools were mentioned for BP management. 3.2. Educational materials. The quality of educational materials varied for BP and smoking management. For BP, patients received post-visit printouts with vital signs, but the use of informational pamphlets was not reported. For smoking care, staff described using smoking cessation folders, but staff often distributed information without further follow-up. For example, one health system described that patients would “get the packet [about quit resources], but it wouldn’t necessarily be a conversation.” [P8] Another nurse reported “sometimes” going through the resource packet, depending on “how interested the patient is.” [P11] As one nurse cautioned, “When you give them this packet of [tobacco cessation] paperwork, it’s kind of like ‘Well, we care a little, but we don’t have time to fully follow-up.’” [P6] Theme 4: Rheumatologists’ and staff’s attitudes and knowledge The fourth theme identified was rheumatologists’ and staff’s knowledge and attitudes, which encompassed the spectrum of rheumatologists’ perceptions of their responsibilities, staff’s confidence in communication with patients, and staff’s knowledge of treatment guidelines. 4.1. Rheumatologists’ perceptions of roles and responsibilities. Staff reported that certain rheumatologists did not see BP or smoking management as their responsibility. For example, one staff member said that a rheumatologist told her “I don’t deal with BP,” [P6] and another multi-specialty nurse was told “That’s not my bag, baby.” [P2] However, staff noted that when rheumatologists saw BP management as a physicians’ obligation, their expanded role became a strategy for follow-up. One nurse described a rheumatologist as “very receptive,” often requesting “Can you put a call in to their primary?” or “Can you counsel the patient?” [P6] These strategies were not described for smoking risk management. 4.2. Staff’s confidence in communication strategies. Staff often expressed trouble finding the appropriate words for smoking cessation conversations—“I don’t have the verbiage to continue” [P2] —and requested talking points to facilitate these patient interactions. In the future, they suggested promoting smoking cessation conversations as standard practice, “That way [patients] know everyone is being asked and they’re not being targeted as a smoker.” [P1] Although most staff requested specific talking points to guide smoking cessation conversations, some staff described proficiency and comfort with these exchanges. One nurse stated “I encourage [cutting back] because obviously they’re trying to make an effort. I think that whole word, ‘Quit,’ is overwhelming to someone.” [P4] These staff also understood the importance of metered language, such as “Is there something that’s preventing you from quitting smoking?” [P6] or “You know what, BP is a little high, you might want to check it out,” [P13] to reflect sensitivity towards patients’ experiences. One nurse described just mentioning high BP as a communication strategy: “[Sharing BP] puts it in their head…and the patient is like ‘Oh and somebody was going to come back and recheck my BP,’ and then they come and get you, but unless you put the bug in the ear, sometimes that doesn’t happen.” [P15] 4.3. Staff’s knowledge about care standards and treatment guidelines. Staff shared varying levels of knowledge on BP interpretation ranges amid guideline changes, asking questions such as “Is [120/80] still textbook?” [P7] and “Age doesn’t matter either?” [P6] Reported knowledge varied among nurses and medical assistants based on prior experiences. Despite several staff reporting former or current smoking, self-reported knowledge about smoking cessation strategies, treatments, and coverage was generally lower. One medical assistant described her understanding as contingent on her personal experience: “Since I’m a smoker, I know what’s out there.” [P6] Theme 5: Staff’s perception of patients’ attitudes and knowledge The fifth theme identified was staff’s perception of patients’ attitudes and knowledge. This was the only theme that did not elicit strategies and exclusively captured barriers. 5.1. Staff’s perception of patient uninterest or discomfort. Many staff described patients as indifferent or reluctant when talking about BP and smoking, respectively. When discussing high BP, clinic staff repeatedly reported that patients were “in denial,” [P16] making “a lot of excuses,” [P7] or insisting that ranges were “normal for me.” [P17] Clinic staff said smoking conversations “just depended on the patient,” [P13] but consistently described patients’ reactions as less accepting. Patient uninterest or discomfort also contributed to staff’s low confidence in communication. In one instance, a nurse reported that a patient was against engaging the Quit Line simply because “She did not want someone calling her out of the blue.” [P1] With BP, patient discomfort led most staff to keep exchanges brief and only mentioned BP “numbers” to avoid lengthy conversations. [P8] 5.2. Staff’s perception of patients’ knowledge about care standards. Staff also described varying levels of patients’ baseline knowledge of BP and smoking management. On a spectrum, nurses reported that many patients were unsure about normal BP ranges but seemed interested in “recording [their BP] on paper and… following it.” [P11] On the other end, nurses reported a small number of patients asked about forwarding results to primary care. One nurse stated that some patients “have asked me ‘Are you going to put this in my chart so my PCP or my regular doctor can see this?’ Some will ask us to write it in their little BP monitor books.” [P1] For smoking, staff more commonly described low knowledge as a barrier. Staff reported that most patients “realize smoking affects lungs… but don’t realize it can worsen their actual [rheumatic] disease and make their symptoms worse.” [P9] Staff also pointed to patients’ limited knowledge of cessation resources. For example, patients “know that there’s nicotine patches or the Chantix [prescription cessation agent], but they don’t know there’s like nicotine inhalers and Wellbutrin [prescription cessation agent] and all kinds of stuff.” [P6] DISCUSSION Our study characterized rheumatology staff’s existing clinic work process for managing BP and smoking, and further identified several barriers and strategies that could serve as both targets for intervention and approaches to emulate. Two pervasive barriers—1) the absence of a standard, structured system for follow-up protocols, both within the specialty clinic and across settings, and 2) low staff confidence in communication strategies—can be addressed by applying existing systems and educational frameworks. Our findings build upon previous studies noting inconsistent or time-consuming interventions for smoking cessation and CVD prevention in rheumatology. Primdahl and colleagues developed a European protocol for nurses to systematically screen rheumatology patients for CVD risk, which addressed both absence of a standardized structure and low staff confidence but required nearly 30 minutes per patient [ 28 ]. Our findings are also similar to a survey study by Naranjo and colleagues where 395 rheumatologists and 199 nurses from 25 countries noted that less than one in five departments had a protocol for smoking cessation [ 29 ]. In contrast, our participants called for systematic and brief interventions. Despite the limited systems-level support structures, rheumatology staff were eager to learn about practices to improve BP and smoking follow-up. Staff consistently identified high BP and smoking but inconsistently followed up with care needs, such as re-checking BP or initiating readiness-to-quit assessments. Cases of fragmented staff-physician communication repeatedly appeared as the root of this problem. For example, staff reported that rooming routine time constraints and inconsistent use of physical reminders often prevented them from following up with high-risk patients. Since staff-physician collaboration has been proven to shape patient health outcomes in many medical settings [ 30 - 32 ], it is critical that rheumatology clinics streamline and prioritize a team-based practice. Task standardization of rooming procedures was also recommended to improve quality and efficiency [ 33 ]. Across settings, staff frequently described uncertainty as to whether they or the rheumatologist should initiate primary care follow-up. In rare cases when staff used the EHR to send notes directly to primary care, staff had no confirmation if primary care even addressed these concerns. Future work may explore establishing primary care communication as a staff role, as they are a first contact in addressing concerns about BP and smoking. This strategy has already demonstrated success in one quasi-experimental cohort study, where a clinic staff-led protocol for smoking cessation increased tobacco quit line referrals 26-fold [ 34 ]. When clinical staff counseled patients about smoking, staff confidence was often influenced by their personal smoking cessation experience. Prior literature suggests that nurses and medical assistants are more likely than physicians to be current or former smokers [ 35 ]. This direct experience has the potential to make them valuable patient partners for cessation counseling if equipped with talking points and action steps, as there is evidence that patients value when clinical staff have a shared personal health experience [ 25 ]. As noted above, MAs in our study who previously smoked informed new language to assess readiness-to-quit “or cut back.” [ 35 , 36 ] Furthermore, when nursing staff are armed with confidence and health promotion practices, they were more likely to engage conversations about lifestyle changes regardless of their history [ 37 ]. Our findings could be relevant beyond rheumatology, particularly for other non-vascular specialty clinics where CVD risk management is critical. In one study, smoking cessation care practices created exclusively for patients with RA were not superior to standard care [ 38 ]. Accordingly, developing future staff rooming protocols might be applicable beyond rheumatology clinics. Further improvement planning may benefit from leveraging frameworks such as the Behaviour Change Wheel [ 39 ]. The highlighted themes map particularly well to the Wheel framework with the three sources of behavior change—capability, motivation, opportunity—and five of the nine intervention functions. For example, staff’s low confidence in communication strategies were subsequently addressed through our BP and Quit Connect interventions leveraging education, training, modeling, and enablement [ 34 ]. As with any study, we acknowledge limitations. First, we recruited from only two health systems, although this did encompass both academic and community practice. Second, this study was conducted in one geographic region and may not represent all staff processes and perceptions. Third, interviews focused on clinic staff, and did not triangulate data sources with physician and patient accounts at this stage. However, we have conducted other studies on physician and patient perspectives for CVD risk management [ 17 , 25 ]. In conclusion, our study characterized existing clinic work processes for rheumatology staff’s BP and smoking risk management practices and provided insights into modifiable barriers and effective strategies. The two most notable barriers were (1) the absence of a structured system for follow-up conversation and care and (2) staff’s confidence in communication to facilitate action. Our findings offer guidance for rheumatology clinics to tailor protocols to routinely incorporate BP and smoking risk management within their practices. Acknowledgments: The authors wish to thank Amanda Perez for manuscript support, as well as clinic staff participants for their valuable insights and collaboration. Financial support: Portions of this project were supported by the Clinical and Translational Science Award (CTSA) program, through the NIH National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS), grant 1UL1TR002373, as well as the University of Wisconsin - Madison School of Medicine and Public Health’s Wisconsin Partnership Program. Work was also supported by peer-reviewed institutional grant funding from Independent Grants for Learning and Change (Pfizer; PI-Bartels). DW is supported by the Gundersen Medical Foundation. Funders played no role in the design, conduct, or interpretation of results. The manuscript content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the funders. Footnotes Conflicts of Interest: All other authors declare no relationships or conflicts of interest. REFERENCES 1. del Rincon ID, Williams K, Stern MP, et al. High incidence of cardiovascular events in a rheumatoid arthritis cohort not explained by traditional cardiac risk factors. Arthritis Rheum. 2001;44(12):2737–45. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Avina-Zubieta JA, Choi HK, Sadatsafavi M, et al. Risk of cardiovascular mortality in patients with rheumatoid arthritis: a meta-analysis of observational studies. Arthritis Rheum. 2008;59(12):1690–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Yurkovich M, Vostretsova K, Chen W, et al. Overall and cause-specific mortality in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus: a meta-analysis of observational studies. Arthritis Care Res (Hoboken). 2014;66(4):608–16. [